1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,980

2
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:07,040
Let's get started.

3
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,200
Today we're going to be
talking about exploration.

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,400
We're going to be starting
by talking about exploration

5
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,960
in standard reinforcement
learning settings,

6
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:18,680
actually, primarily,
in bandit settings,

7
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:20,640
and then how that might
translate to robotics

8
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:21,853
and large language models.

9
00:00:21,853 --> 00:00:23,520
And then we're actually
going to revisit

10
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:25,728
some of the meta-learning
topics that we talked about

11
00:00:25,728 --> 00:00:29,800
on Wednesday and revisit how
we might effectively learn how

12
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:33,800
to explore in meta-RL settings.

13
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,600
So what is the
exploration problem?

14
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:41,000
If we apply reinforcement
learning to some problems,

15
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:42,500
we're able to solve
them quite well.

16
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,400
So for example, if we try to
train a reinforcement learning

17
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:48,560
agent to play Breakout,
where the reward is

18
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:53,920
the score of the game, you get a
pretty performant player that's

19
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:57,800
able to get a high score.

20
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:02,130
But if you instead try to train
a reinforcement learning agent

21
00:01:02,130 --> 00:01:04,730
to play a game like this
one on the right, which

22
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:08,370
is called Montezuma's
Revenge, it's

23
00:01:08,370 --> 00:01:10,970
really, really hard for
reinforcement learning systems

24
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:13,450
to play.

25
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:15,890
Now, why is there
this difference?

26
00:01:15,890 --> 00:01:21,975
So in the second game, you can
get a reward if you get a key.

27
00:01:21,975 --> 00:01:23,850
Kind of intuitively, if
you're getting a key,

28
00:01:23,850 --> 00:01:27,090
that's useful for
being able to progress.

29
00:01:27,090 --> 00:01:29,730
Opening a door is also useful.

30
00:01:29,730 --> 00:01:33,350
And there's this skull
that's rolling around.

31
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:36,490
And intuitively,
we as humans know

32
00:01:36,490 --> 00:01:38,970
that that's probably something
you might want to avoid.

33
00:01:38,970 --> 00:01:42,850
Likewise, falling off of
a ledge might be something

34
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:43,670
that you'd avoid.

35
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:47,050
But these are things that the
agent doesn't know itself.

36
00:01:47,050 --> 00:01:51,210
And in general, kind
of completing the game,

37
00:01:51,210 --> 00:01:53,890
these are very sparse,
discrete events.

38
00:01:53,890 --> 00:01:56,850
And completing the game
only weakly correlates

39
00:01:56,850 --> 00:01:59,860
relates with those
rewarding events.

40
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:02,500
And so actually, each of those
individual rewarding events

41
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:04,860
are also kind of
few and far between.

42
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:06,380
So for example,
at the beginning,

43
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:09,860
the agent isn't getting
any reward at all

44
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:12,500
and it has to figure out
how to even get to the key

45
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:14,380
in the first place,
which requires quite

46
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:17,740
a long sequence of events.

47
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:20,180
Now, as humans, we
kind of know what

48
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:22,500
to do because we
understand what each

49
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:26,020
of these sprites
in the game mean.

50
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:28,420
But the algorithm really
doesn't know that.

51
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:30,700
So if we instead try
to imagine ourselves

52
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:33,140
as reinforcement
learning algorithms,

53
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:34,760
let's think through
that a little bit.

54
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:39,180
And we can think through
that with a game called Mao.

55
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:44,566
It's like half a game,
half a joke in some ways.

56
00:02:44,566 --> 00:02:47,980
You aren't told the rules
of the game in advance.

57
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:52,093
And if you break a rule,
then you get a penalty.

58
00:02:52,093 --> 00:02:53,260
And you don't actually know.

59
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:56,730
You get a penalty, but you don't
actually know what the rule is,

60
00:02:56,730 --> 00:02:59,790
and you have to discover the
rules through trial and error.

61
00:02:59,790 --> 00:03:02,230
And when you get a penalty,
it's not always clear

62
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:03,910
why you got a penalty.

63
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:09,030
The rules don't actually
necessarily make sense to you.

64
00:03:09,030 --> 00:03:12,750
And tasks like
Montezuma's Revenge

65
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:17,170
are difficult because
reinforcement learning methods,

66
00:03:17,170 --> 00:03:18,690
they don't know about the rules.

67
00:03:18,690 --> 00:03:20,910
They know very little
about what's good

68
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:24,070
and what's bad, what they
can do, what they can't do.

69
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:28,190
And when tasks become
longer and longer,

70
00:03:28,190 --> 00:03:31,210
this also makes the problem
even more difficult,

71
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:33,950
because you have to figure
out an even longer sequence

72
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:36,710
of actions that might
lead to getting reward

73
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:39,550
versus getting a penalty.

74
00:03:39,550 --> 00:03:42,670
And so if you think about what
the reinforcement learning

75
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:45,430
agent has to do,
reinforcement learning agents

76
00:03:45,430 --> 00:03:48,750
are kind of analogous to
imagining if your goal in life

77
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:54,120
was to win 50 games of Mao,
and you also didn't even

78
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:55,560
know this in advance.

79
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:59,220
You only kind of learned this
from actually getting rewards,

80
00:03:59,220 --> 00:04:04,120
from winning games and getting
penalties from losing games.

81
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:08,000
So that's what the
reinforcement learning system--

82
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,820
that's the difficulty that
these algorithms are faced with.

83
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:13,400
It's really, really
hard, especially

84
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:16,839
in sparse-reward scenarios.

85
00:04:16,839 --> 00:04:21,360
Now, if we were to think about
this exploration problem,

86
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:24,600
there's a couple different
ways of looking at it.

87
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:28,440
One way of looking at it is,
we want to discover high reward

88
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,120
strategies.

89
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:36,600
We want to basically seek out
rewards and find sequences

90
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:39,080
of actions that, individually,
those actions may not

91
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:41,300
be rewarding, but when
we seek them together,

92
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:44,840
we're able to find rewards.

93
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:49,440
And another way to view
the exploration problem

94
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:52,740
is deciding whether to
attempt a new behavior,

95
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:57,490
or continuing to do the best
thing that it knows so far.

96
00:04:57,490 --> 00:04:59,740
You're basically deciding,
should I try something new,

97
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:04,700
or should I do something that
has been good in the past?

98
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:07,780
These are actually part
of a single problem,

99
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:10,740
where you need to--

100
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:12,320
there's this
exploitation problem,

101
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:15,320
which is doing what you know
will yield the highest reward,

102
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:18,540
doing the things that
you've seen before that have

103
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:20,600
high reward, acting
according to your Q function,

104
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:24,080
for example, and this
exploration problem,

105
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:27,020
which is doing things that you
haven't done before in hopes

106
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:30,128
of getting higher reward.

107
00:05:30,128 --> 00:05:32,420
And so that's how we can
think about this reinforcement

108
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:34,587
learning problem, where we
need to balance these two

109
00:05:34,587 --> 00:05:37,300
objectives if our
goal is to maximize

110
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:39,490
a sum of rewards over time.

111
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:42,540

112
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:47,320
As some examples, if
you are in a new city,

113
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:50,430
you move somewhere
new, exploration

114
00:05:50,430 --> 00:05:52,138
would be trying new restaurants.

115
00:05:52,138 --> 00:05:54,430
Exploitation would be going
to your favorite restaurant

116
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:58,230
or restaurant that
you like the best.

117
00:05:58,230 --> 00:06:00,830
One actually very common use
of reinforcement learning

118
00:06:00,830 --> 00:06:05,950
is figuring out which
ads to place and where.

119
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:09,070
And exploitation would
be taking the ad that

120
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:13,830
has been the most successful and
showing that one to the user.

121
00:06:13,830 --> 00:06:15,590
An exploration would
be instead, trying

122
00:06:15,590 --> 00:06:18,030
to pick a different
advertisement at random,

123
00:06:18,030 --> 00:06:21,980
and instead showing that
advertisement to the user.

124
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:24,550

125
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:27,550
Or as a kind of a final
example, in oil drilling,

126
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:30,638
you don't know where
you're going to find--

127
00:06:30,638 --> 00:06:31,930
where you're going to find oil.

128
00:06:31,930 --> 00:06:34,350
And so exploitation would
be taking the best location

129
00:06:34,350 --> 00:06:36,990
that you've found before
that has yielded oil,

130
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:39,320
versus exploration would be
drilling at a new location.

131
00:06:39,320 --> 00:06:44,054

132
00:06:44,054 --> 00:06:46,910
So exploration is quite hard.

133
00:06:46,910 --> 00:06:51,420
And if we want to design an
algorithm for exploration,

134
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:55,000
we might think about what is
the best strategy for exploring

135
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,120
and the best way to trade
off between exploration

136
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:59,440
and exploitation.

137
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:01,880
And we also need to
think about, what

138
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:05,800
does optimality of
exploration even mean?

139
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:09,720
There's a range of
reinforcement learning problems,

140
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:11,760
ranging from more
simple problems

141
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:14,920
where you can actually
analyze whether or not

142
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:19,320
the strategy is optimal or not,
to more complicated problems

143
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:23,940
where it's intractable to
analyze whether or it's optimal,

144
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:26,280
and also intractable
to develop algorithms

145
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:30,620
that are able to do a
good job at exploration.

146
00:07:30,620 --> 00:07:35,200
On the simple side, you start
with multi-armed bandits,

147
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:39,120
the kind of one-step RL problems
that don't have any state.

148
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:41,620
And then if you want to
make that more complicated,

149
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:42,660
you add context.

150
00:07:42,660 --> 00:07:46,850
You add some sort of features
for each POS for the state

151
00:07:46,850 --> 00:07:47,710
that you're in.

152
00:07:47,710 --> 00:07:51,850
You could then move to
small MDPs, to larger MDPs.

153
00:07:51,850 --> 00:07:54,897
And a lot of the
kind of problems

154
00:07:54,897 --> 00:07:56,730
that we've been talking
about in this course

155
00:07:56,730 --> 00:07:59,150
are large MDPs without
any known structure,

156
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:03,205
and those are the ones that are
like the hardest for getting

157
00:08:03,205 --> 00:08:05,330
good exploration strategy,
and also, theoretically,

158
00:08:05,330 --> 00:08:09,290
thinking about exploration.

159
00:08:09,290 --> 00:08:13,290
So before we talk
about exploration

160
00:08:13,290 --> 00:08:15,330
in the large problems,
let's talk a little bit

161
00:08:15,330 --> 00:08:18,650
about bandits for a minute,
because they provide a nice way

162
00:08:18,650 --> 00:08:19,990
to think about exploration.

163
00:08:19,990 --> 00:08:23,010
And they're actually
used in practice

164
00:08:23,010 --> 00:08:25,410
for a variety of problems.

165
00:08:25,410 --> 00:08:29,450
And specifically, the way
that we can think about--

166
00:08:29,450 --> 00:08:30,890
what is a bandit?

167
00:08:30,890 --> 00:08:35,330
It's not the kind of someone
that runs around and maybe tries

168
00:08:35,330 --> 00:08:36,929
to steal stuff or something.

169
00:08:36,929 --> 00:08:43,980
It's a formulation of
reinforcement learning,

170
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:52,500
where the horizon is just one
time step for a single episode.

171
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:55,740
There is no state.

172
00:08:55,740 --> 00:09:00,820
And unlike everything we've
talked about in MDP so far,

173
00:09:00,820 --> 00:09:02,720
your reward is
actually stochastic.

174
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:07,140

175
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:11,205
And so if you go back
to the examples before,

176
00:09:11,205 --> 00:09:12,580
you could think
about the problem

177
00:09:12,580 --> 00:09:16,420
as selecting a restaurant
as a bandit problem where

178
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:23,280
you could, for example, select
to go to one restaurant.

179
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:25,720
You could select to go to
a different restaurant.

180
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:28,620
You could select to go to
a different restaurant,

181
00:09:28,620 --> 00:09:30,380
and so forth.

182
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:32,820
And depending on your
experience in previous episodes

183
00:09:32,820 --> 00:09:36,397
of selecting restaurants,
you could inform your choice

184
00:09:36,397 --> 00:09:38,980
about whether you want to pick
a new restaurant where you know

185
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:42,630
nothing about the reward,
or go to a restaurant

186
00:09:42,630 --> 00:09:47,470
that you've received high
reward for it in the future.

187
00:09:47,470 --> 00:09:49,670
And the reward is
stochastic in the sense

188
00:09:49,670 --> 00:09:52,710
that your experience
at that restaurant

189
00:09:52,710 --> 00:09:55,750
may not be the same
every single time.

190
00:09:55,750 --> 00:10:01,470
And so yeah, this is what a
bandit problem looks like.

191
00:10:01,470 --> 00:10:06,510
Now, if we want to think
about how good is exploration

192
00:10:06,510 --> 00:10:08,390
and how good is
our reinforcement

193
00:10:08,390 --> 00:10:11,750
learning algorithm,
we want to think

194
00:10:11,750 --> 00:10:18,510
about how much reward we're
getting relative to if we

195
00:10:18,510 --> 00:10:20,950
were acting optimally.

196
00:10:20,950 --> 00:10:27,310
And so far, we've been
thinking about sums of rewards,

197
00:10:27,310 --> 00:10:29,650
oftentime averaged
over episodes.

198
00:10:29,650 --> 00:10:32,230

199
00:10:32,230 --> 00:10:34,217
In bandit problems,
you actually are

200
00:10:34,217 --> 00:10:36,550
often thinking about, actually,
the entire reinforcement

201
00:10:36,550 --> 00:10:39,110
learning process, and the
optimality of the reinforcement

202
00:10:39,110 --> 00:10:41,480
learning process, and the
optimality of exploration

203
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:43,280
followed by exploitation.

204
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:53,120
And so say that you have taken
capital T episodes so far.

205
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:56,200
Then the best you
could have done

206
00:10:56,200 --> 00:11:03,840
is going to be something like
the expected reward of taking

207
00:11:03,840 --> 00:11:10,680
the optimal action times T. And
this is actually in some ways

208
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:15,000
an oracle, because when you
haven't taken any episodes

209
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,440
at the beginning,
then you are forced

210
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:21,280
to take a random action
because you don't know anything

211
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:23,200
about the MDP so far.

212
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:24,980
And so this is kind
of, in retrospect,

213
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:27,360
what is the best thing
you could have done.

214
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:32,360
And this is a upper bound
on the sum of rewards

215
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:36,440
that you could be
getting after T episodes.

216
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:40,110
And then if we want to think
about how well we've been doing,

217
00:11:40,110 --> 00:11:43,450
we can think about this
subtracted by the sum of rewards

218
00:11:43,450 --> 00:11:46,770
that we actually observed from
the actions that we've taken.

219
00:11:46,770 --> 00:11:50,250
And so that would be
something like a sum from T

220
00:11:50,250 --> 00:11:56,210
equals 1 to T of the
action-- the actual action

221
00:11:56,210 --> 00:12:02,410
that we decided to take in
each particular episode.

222
00:12:02,410 --> 00:12:07,582
And the gap between these
two is going to be measuring,

223
00:12:07,582 --> 00:12:09,930
basically, a form
of optimality gap.

224
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:14,850
And this expression is
something that's called regret.

225
00:12:14,850 --> 00:12:18,210
And it's measuring,
basically, how much

226
00:12:18,210 --> 00:12:20,810
do we regret taking the
action that we did compared

227
00:12:20,810 --> 00:12:22,240
to taking the optimal action.

228
00:12:22,240 --> 00:12:30,210

229
00:12:30,210 --> 00:12:34,730
Now, if you were to think
about regret over time,

230
00:12:34,730 --> 00:12:37,340
so here, actually, time
is indexing over episodes

231
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:40,940
because each episode is
just a single time step.

232
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:45,380
So if we think
about as a function

233
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:51,580
of the number of
episodes, the regret

234
00:12:51,580 --> 00:12:55,780
after taking those
number of episodes.

235
00:12:55,780 --> 00:13:00,700
If you were acting
optimally, what sort of curve

236
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:05,020
would you get on this plot?

237
00:13:05,020 --> 00:13:09,680
Yeah, so I see mostly
going straight.

238
00:13:09,680 --> 00:13:12,500
So if you're acting
optimally, this will be zero,

239
00:13:12,500 --> 00:13:15,880
and it will be zero even as you
have more and more episodes.

240
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:17,460
It'll be zero in expectation.

241
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:20,340
There might be some randomness.

242
00:13:20,340 --> 00:13:26,340
And so you would get a
curve that looks like this.

243
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:31,580
And so this is sort of optimal.

244
00:13:31,580 --> 00:13:33,740
Now, in practice,
actually achieving

245
00:13:33,740 --> 00:13:37,495
this is not really possible
because at the first time step,

246
00:13:37,495 --> 00:13:39,620
like I mentioned, you have
to take a random action.

247
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:43,390

248
00:13:43,390 --> 00:13:46,510
Now, does anyone have thoughts
on what this curve would

249
00:13:46,510 --> 00:13:51,510
look like if you were just
taking an episode at random.

250
00:13:51,510 --> 00:13:52,730
Yeah, it'll keep increasing.

251
00:13:52,730 --> 00:13:53,430
Yeah.

252
00:13:53,430 --> 00:13:57,696
So if you're taking
an episode at random,

253
00:13:57,696 --> 00:13:59,283
there will now be a
gap between these,

254
00:13:59,283 --> 00:14:00,950
and you would be
getting a higher reward

255
00:14:00,950 --> 00:14:03,710
from taking the optimal
action compared to taking

256
00:14:03,710 --> 00:14:05,510
that action at random.

257
00:14:05,510 --> 00:14:12,230
And if, for example, you
were always picking A1,

258
00:14:12,230 --> 00:14:16,710
and A1 is not the best action,
then after one episode,

259
00:14:16,710 --> 00:14:24,670
you would expect to have
some kind of non-zero regret.

260
00:14:24,670 --> 00:14:26,850
So you would have a curve
that looks like this.

261
00:14:26,850 --> 00:14:30,590
And then if you took A1
again the next episode,

262
00:14:30,590 --> 00:14:37,970
then you would then
incur the same difference

263
00:14:37,970 --> 00:14:42,630
in optimal rewards versus
the rewards that you took.

264
00:14:42,630 --> 00:14:46,170
And so then this would
increase, and you would actually

265
00:14:46,170 --> 00:14:48,970
get something that looks linear.

266
00:14:48,970 --> 00:14:52,410
And that means that,
basically, every time you--

267
00:14:52,410 --> 00:14:54,090
basically, at every
time step, there's

268
00:14:54,090 --> 00:14:58,170
a fixed difference between the
optimal reward and the reward

269
00:14:58,170 --> 00:14:59,490
that you took.

270
00:14:59,490 --> 00:15:05,150
And so whenever you get a linear
regret increasing linearly,

271
00:15:05,150 --> 00:15:09,090
that means that your algorithm
is not actually improving.

272
00:15:09,090 --> 00:15:11,050
It's not actually
using the exploration

273
00:15:11,050 --> 00:15:12,360
to get better rewards.

274
00:15:12,360 --> 00:15:15,130

275
00:15:15,130 --> 00:15:19,570
If instead you were--

276
00:15:19,570 --> 00:15:22,510
the blue markers never worked
that well, but we can see.

277
00:15:22,510 --> 00:15:25,090
If instead you were actually
having an algorithm that

278
00:15:25,090 --> 00:15:29,670
is a bit smarter, then you would
expect to have sublinear regret.

279
00:15:29,670 --> 00:15:32,860
And so you'd expect actually
not just linearly increase

280
00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:34,380
your regret over
time, but actually

281
00:15:34,380 --> 00:15:37,060
have this curve look
something like this,

282
00:15:37,060 --> 00:15:41,560
where it starts looking
something more like linear.

283
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:45,220
And then eventually, if you
then get to the optimal behavior

284
00:15:45,220 --> 00:15:48,300
after exploring,
then it flattens out

285
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:49,600
to something like this.

286
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:55,220
And you could imagine that
this portion is like you're now

287
00:15:55,220 --> 00:15:57,420
in the exploitation phase.

288
00:15:57,420 --> 00:15:59,180
And the phase
before that is when

289
00:15:59,180 --> 00:16:04,900
you're exploring and gathering
information about the problem.

290
00:16:04,900 --> 00:16:07,280
The question is, to
make these curves,

291
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:09,780
are we running the learning
process a lot of different times

292
00:16:09,780 --> 00:16:12,435
and then averaging?

293
00:16:12,435 --> 00:16:14,060
So for these curves,
this is actually--

294
00:16:14,060 --> 00:16:18,220
each of these curves is just one
reinforcement learning process.

295
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:21,800
And I guess they're
very smooth here,

296
00:16:21,800 --> 00:16:26,140
and in practice, they'll
be a little bit noisier.

297
00:16:26,140 --> 00:16:29,850
The optimal, I guess, this
is kind of an expectation.

298
00:16:29,850 --> 00:16:32,610
And so this is
going to be smooth.

299
00:16:32,610 --> 00:16:36,990
But you might have something
where, for example, in reality,

300
00:16:36,990 --> 00:16:40,310
maybe you got unlucky
one time step.

301
00:16:40,310 --> 00:16:44,567
And this went up
because you got unlucky.

302
00:16:44,567 --> 00:16:47,150
Maybe at the next time step, you
still took the optimal action

303
00:16:47,150 --> 00:16:49,390
but got lucky.

304
00:16:49,390 --> 00:16:53,550
And so this will actually have
some noise in it in practice.

305
00:16:53,550 --> 00:16:55,550
Yeah, so the question is,
we have an expectation

306
00:16:55,550 --> 00:16:56,250
on the first part.

307
00:16:56,250 --> 00:16:57,667
We don't have an
expectation here.

308
00:16:57,667 --> 00:17:00,256
Why is that?

309
00:17:00,256 --> 00:17:03,550
This is basically some measure
of the optimal expected

310
00:17:03,550 --> 00:17:06,950
reward after T time steps.

311
00:17:06,950 --> 00:17:10,050
This is actually just the
actual reward that you observed.

312
00:17:10,050 --> 00:17:13,080
You could also have an
expectation over this,

313
00:17:13,080 --> 00:17:15,190
over reinforcement
learning runs.

314
00:17:15,190 --> 00:17:19,955
And that would then give you--

315
00:17:19,955 --> 00:17:22,935
as an example here, if you run
this algorithm multiple times,

316
00:17:22,935 --> 00:17:24,310
these regret curves
will actually

317
00:17:24,310 --> 00:17:25,852
look a little bit
different depending

318
00:17:25,852 --> 00:17:26,829
on how unlucky you get.

319
00:17:26,829 --> 00:17:29,000
And then if you
were to then measure

320
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:30,920
this in terms of
expectations, then that

321
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:33,200
would be averaging
over different runs

322
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:35,720
of the reinforcement
learning algorithm.

323
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:39,360
Yeah, so the question is, if
we're taking random actions,

324
00:17:39,360 --> 00:17:43,240
why is it linear, and why is it
increasing by a fixed amount?

325
00:17:43,240 --> 00:17:49,250
So this will be noisy if you
are taking random actions.

326
00:17:49,250 --> 00:17:54,572

327
00:17:54,572 --> 00:17:55,780
I don't know how to say that.

328
00:17:55,780 --> 00:17:58,400

329
00:17:58,400 --> 00:17:59,820
So yeah, it will be noisy.

330
00:17:59,820 --> 00:18:03,720
If you run it multiple times in
expectation, it will be linear.

331
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:06,720
And if you have a
deterministic MDP

332
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:09,880
and you always select
action 1 through a very not

333
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:14,000
smart strategy, and the rewards
are a bit more deterministic,

334
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
then you might actually get
a curve that looks like this.

335
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,820
So the question is, if
we're always taking A1,

336
00:18:19,820 --> 00:18:24,000
then how do we actually know
how to measure this formula?

337
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,510
This is not actually
a formula that--

338
00:18:26,510 --> 00:18:28,730
sorry.

339
00:18:28,730 --> 00:18:30,690
To back up a little bit,
this is actually not--

340
00:18:30,690 --> 00:18:34,035
this is what we're aiming to--
we're aiming to minimize regret.

341
00:18:34,035 --> 00:18:35,410
And we're aiming
to get something

342
00:18:35,410 --> 00:18:37,130
that is able to flatten out.

343
00:18:37,130 --> 00:18:40,490
But this is more of an object
that's used for analysis

344
00:18:40,490 --> 00:18:42,470
and not our
optimization objective,

345
00:18:42,470 --> 00:18:46,210
because we actually
can't measure this

346
00:18:46,210 --> 00:18:49,570
without knowledge of
the bandit problem,

347
00:18:49,570 --> 00:18:51,650
or knowledge of the MDP.

348
00:18:51,650 --> 00:18:53,590
If you introduce an
expectation here,

349
00:18:53,590 --> 00:18:55,970
then you would expect
it to be monotonic.

350
00:18:55,970 --> 00:19:00,050
If not, then because the
reward is stochastic,

351
00:19:00,050 --> 00:19:03,450
you could have
scenarios where you

352
00:19:03,450 --> 00:19:12,210
say that the best action
is A5, and the probability

353
00:19:12,210 --> 00:19:14,970
of getting good reward is--

354
00:19:14,970 --> 00:19:18,090
I don't know-- 90% of the
time, and 10% of the time,

355
00:19:18,090 --> 00:19:19,330
you get a bad reward.

356
00:19:19,330 --> 00:19:24,120
Then you're going to see,
when you get a good reward,

357
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:29,780
it will actually relative to the
expert which is averaging over

358
00:19:29,780 --> 00:19:31,440
that, it will go down.

359
00:19:31,440 --> 00:19:33,732
But if you write this
as an expectation

360
00:19:33,732 --> 00:19:35,440
for the reinforcement
learning algorithm,

361
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:37,660
it would be monotonic.

362
00:19:37,660 --> 00:19:38,620
Cool.

363
00:19:38,620 --> 00:19:42,340
So this is the regret
formula, regret

364
00:19:42,340 --> 00:19:45,340
after doing reinforcement
learning for T episodes.

365
00:19:45,340 --> 00:19:47,320
Expected reward of
the best action,

366
00:19:47,320 --> 00:19:49,620
basically, the best we can
hope for in expectation,

367
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:55,352
if we had knowledge
of what the bandit is.

368
00:19:55,352 --> 00:19:57,060
And then this is the
reward of the action

369
00:19:57,060 --> 00:20:00,380
that's actually taken.

370
00:20:00,380 --> 00:20:03,700
There's a variety of simple
strategies for exploration,

371
00:20:03,700 --> 00:20:09,300
and including some that you
can show that are optimal.

372
00:20:09,300 --> 00:20:11,180
And you can often
analyze algorithms

373
00:20:11,180 --> 00:20:13,550
and provide theoretical
guarantees on regret.

374
00:20:13,550 --> 00:20:18,100

375
00:20:18,100 --> 00:20:19,900
And then also,
even the algorithms

376
00:20:19,900 --> 00:20:23,030
that you can theoretically
make some guarantees

377
00:20:23,030 --> 00:20:27,750
about in practice,
performance can vary.

378
00:20:27,750 --> 00:20:30,952
And so to get a little bit
more intuition-- actually, no.

379
00:20:30,952 --> 00:20:33,410
Before we do that-- we're going
to play a game in a minute,

380
00:20:33,410 --> 00:20:34,785
but before we do
that, let's talk

381
00:20:34,785 --> 00:20:37,670
about different strategies
for exploration,

382
00:20:37,670 --> 00:20:44,430
so specifically in
bandits, so not in MDPs.

383
00:20:44,430 --> 00:20:47,450
So one approach is when you're
selecting different restaurants,

384
00:20:47,450 --> 00:20:52,070
for example, if you haven't
explored a restaurant,

385
00:20:52,070 --> 00:20:54,630
then you could choose
to be optimistic

386
00:20:54,630 --> 00:20:56,270
about that restaurant.

387
00:20:56,270 --> 00:20:59,550
And that will incentivize you
to explore new restaurants,

388
00:20:59,550 --> 00:21:02,390
rather than always being greedy
with respect to restaurants

389
00:21:02,390 --> 00:21:04,630
you've tried before.

390
00:21:04,630 --> 00:21:06,150
And so the way
this could work is

391
00:21:06,150 --> 00:21:07,870
you could track
the average reward

392
00:21:07,870 --> 00:21:13,230
that you have received in the
past for each of the actions.

393
00:21:13,230 --> 00:21:15,710
And then if you
were to exploit, you

394
00:21:15,710 --> 00:21:18,590
would just take
the action that has

395
00:21:18,590 --> 00:21:22,000
the maximum average
reward for the things

396
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:23,880
that you've seen so far.

397
00:21:23,880 --> 00:21:27,200
But instead, if we want to
actually encourage the algorithm

398
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:30,400
to visit new things,
then we could--

399
00:21:30,400 --> 00:21:32,560
we still want to
visit actions that we

400
00:21:32,560 --> 00:21:34,480
think have high average
rewards, so that if we

401
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:36,100
go to a restaurant
that's really bad,

402
00:21:36,100 --> 00:21:37,880
we maybe don't go to it again.

403
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:42,120
But we're going to add a term
that is some kind of variance

404
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:44,560
of our reward estimate.

405
00:21:44,560 --> 00:21:47,300
This is basically like,
if this variance is high,

406
00:21:47,300 --> 00:21:49,640
that means that we're
uncertain about the reward

407
00:21:49,640 --> 00:21:52,080
for that action.

408
00:21:52,080 --> 00:21:53,840
And if we're
uncertain, then we're

409
00:21:53,840 --> 00:21:56,100
going to basically give
this sort of bonus,

410
00:21:56,100 --> 00:21:58,280
this sort of reward
bonus, on top

411
00:21:58,280 --> 00:22:00,380
of our estimate of
the average reward

412
00:22:00,380 --> 00:22:03,360
to encourage our
algorithm to explore

413
00:22:03,360 --> 00:22:07,375
things that are uncertain, or
things that have high variance.

414
00:22:07,375 --> 00:22:09,000
And so the intuition
here is that we're

415
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:12,400
assuming that unknown things
are going to be good until we

416
00:22:12,400 --> 00:22:14,380
actually then observe them.

417
00:22:14,380 --> 00:22:16,740
And if we then observe them
and they're really bad,

418
00:22:16,740 --> 00:22:19,382
then we'll act accordingly.

419
00:22:19,382 --> 00:22:21,090
In the future, the
variance will go down,

420
00:22:21,090 --> 00:22:22,890
and the reward
estimate will go down.

421
00:22:22,890 --> 00:22:28,072
And this is a way to balance
exploration and exploitation.

422
00:22:28,072 --> 00:22:30,530
Yeah, so the question is, how
would you choose in this case

423
00:22:30,530 --> 00:22:32,410
an action that you
haven't seen before?

424
00:22:32,410 --> 00:22:34,610
So if you haven't
seen an action before,

425
00:22:34,610 --> 00:22:38,250
then your variance estimate
will be something like infinite,

426
00:22:38,250 --> 00:22:39,650
for example.

427
00:22:39,650 --> 00:22:45,730
And then this will encourage
it to explore, basically,

428
00:22:45,730 --> 00:22:47,930
all of the actions that
it hasn't explored first

429
00:22:47,930 --> 00:22:49,570
at least once.

430
00:22:49,570 --> 00:22:51,290
And then from
there, your variance

431
00:22:51,290 --> 00:22:54,290
will go-- your variance
estimate or your uncertainty

432
00:22:54,290 --> 00:22:56,970
about an action will
go down over time.

433
00:22:56,970 --> 00:23:00,330
And yeah, so this is basically,
if you haven't done anything

434
00:23:00,330 --> 00:23:05,130
before, it will incentivize
it to visit that first.

435
00:23:05,130 --> 00:23:05,850
Cool.

436
00:23:05,850 --> 00:23:08,030
So this is a popular algorithm.

437
00:23:08,030 --> 00:23:10,717
You can actually derive
guarantees for it.

438
00:23:10,717 --> 00:23:13,050
It's called upper confidence
bound, or one version of it

439
00:23:13,050 --> 00:23:14,815
is called upper
confidence bound.

440
00:23:14,815 --> 00:23:16,190
There's another
approach that you

441
00:23:16,190 --> 00:23:21,530
can also take that is actually
harder to show guarantees for,

442
00:23:21,530 --> 00:23:24,030
but can work pretty
well in practice.

443
00:23:24,030 --> 00:23:26,642
And we actually saw something
like this in the last lecture.

444
00:23:26,642 --> 00:23:28,350
And essentially, what
you're trying to do

445
00:23:28,350 --> 00:23:31,470
is you're trying to estimate
the probability of getting

446
00:23:31,470 --> 00:23:36,830
good rewards from each of
the arms of the bandit.

447
00:23:36,830 --> 00:23:39,070
And so the way
this would work is

448
00:23:39,070 --> 00:23:44,070
you assume that there's some
sort of distribution of rewards

449
00:23:44,070 --> 00:23:47,070
for each of the actions.

450
00:23:47,070 --> 00:23:52,470
And this defines a
partially observed MDP

451
00:23:52,470 --> 00:23:55,070
where you don't know the
parameters of the reward

452
00:23:55,070 --> 00:23:55,570
function.

453
00:23:55,570 --> 00:23:57,510
You don't know if
you're going to get

454
00:23:57,510 --> 00:24:00,030
a good reward or bad reward.

455
00:24:00,030 --> 00:24:02,390
And so you'll then actually
try to model and form

456
00:24:02,390 --> 00:24:05,830
an estimate of the
likelihood of getting rewards

457
00:24:05,830 --> 00:24:08,230
by trying to model these
parameters in your uncertainty

458
00:24:08,230 --> 00:24:10,483
over it.

459
00:24:10,483 --> 00:24:12,150
So you'll form an
estimate with any data

460
00:24:12,150 --> 00:24:14,110
that you've seen so far.

461
00:24:14,110 --> 00:24:18,920
Then you'll actually
sample an MDP

462
00:24:18,920 --> 00:24:22,960
from your model of
possible MDPs of the world,

463
00:24:22,960 --> 00:24:25,420
and then you'll take
the optimal action,

464
00:24:25,420 --> 00:24:28,960
assuming that your
sample was correct,

465
00:24:28,960 --> 00:24:32,520
and then repeat that process
to update your beliefs again,

466
00:24:32,520 --> 00:24:38,080
and then sample a new MDP,
and repeat the process.

467
00:24:38,080 --> 00:24:39,980
And we'll walk
through an example.

468
00:24:39,980 --> 00:24:42,520
But the intuition here is
that you can iteratively

469
00:24:42,520 --> 00:24:47,200
improve your model of the
bandit and act according

470
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:50,360
to one of the models
that is covered

471
00:24:50,360 --> 00:24:55,280
by one of the potential
sets of probabilities that's

472
00:24:55,280 --> 00:24:58,880
modeled by your MDP model.

473
00:24:58,880 --> 00:25:00,720
One last note,
these are actually

474
00:25:00,720 --> 00:25:03,880
methods that are
widely used and studied

475
00:25:03,880 --> 00:25:08,170
in industry for recommenders
and ad placement.

476
00:25:08,170 --> 00:25:09,920
Everything we've been
talking about so far

477
00:25:09,920 --> 00:25:13,070
is for these sorts of
small bandit problems,

478
00:25:13,070 --> 00:25:16,130
but they are actually
quite relevant for a number

479
00:25:16,130 --> 00:25:20,930
of practical problems, even when
you don't have longer episodes

480
00:25:20,930 --> 00:25:23,970
or episodes with state.

481
00:25:23,970 --> 00:25:26,090
And now, let's
play a little game

482
00:25:26,090 --> 00:25:28,270
to get some intuition
for how this works.

483
00:25:28,270 --> 00:25:35,090
So here is a little simulation.

484
00:25:35,090 --> 00:25:38,410
Say that you are hired as
the head of drug development

485
00:25:38,410 --> 00:25:41,050
at a prestigious
research hospital.

486
00:25:41,050 --> 00:25:48,108
Things are going well, and now
there's some sort of plague,

487
00:25:48,108 --> 00:25:49,650
and you need to
figure out what drugs

488
00:25:49,650 --> 00:25:52,590
are going to be effective
for addressing the plague.

489
00:25:52,590 --> 00:25:56,250
And you want to save
people's lives by giving them

490
00:25:56,250 --> 00:25:59,770
the drug that's most effective.

491
00:25:59,770 --> 00:26:05,390
Now, you can recognize this as a
bandit problem, where each drug,

492
00:26:05,390 --> 00:26:08,730
you don't know how
capable it's going to be.

493
00:26:08,730 --> 00:26:11,400
And we can say that
maybe there is--

494
00:26:11,400 --> 00:26:13,660
maybe there's somewhere
around 5,000 patients,

495
00:26:13,660 --> 00:26:15,980
and there's maybe around
10 different drugs that

496
00:26:15,980 --> 00:26:17,780
you're considering.

497
00:26:17,780 --> 00:26:21,820
And your goal is to try
to understand-- well, try

498
00:26:21,820 --> 00:26:26,860
to save lives, try to save
as many lives as possible,

499
00:26:26,860 --> 00:26:32,317
but also balancing saving lives
right now versus saving lives

500
00:26:32,317 --> 00:26:33,900
in the future as you
learn information

501
00:26:33,900 --> 00:26:36,980
about each of the drugs.

502
00:26:36,980 --> 00:26:38,340
Cool.

503
00:26:38,340 --> 00:26:40,700
So let's play.

504
00:26:40,700 --> 00:26:43,540
Who wants to suggest a drug?

505
00:26:43,540 --> 00:26:44,206
Drug one.

506
00:26:44,206 --> 00:26:44,900
OK.

507
00:26:44,900 --> 00:26:47,240
So we see that we
prescribe drug one.

508
00:26:47,240 --> 00:26:50,180
The patient lived,
so that's good.

509
00:26:50,180 --> 00:26:52,320
Who wants to pick a drug?

510
00:26:52,320 --> 00:26:52,820
Yeah?

511
00:26:52,820 --> 00:26:53,640
Four.

512
00:26:53,640 --> 00:26:54,140
Four.

513
00:26:54,140 --> 00:26:55,550
OK.

514
00:26:55,550 --> 00:26:56,800
We also see the patient lived.

515
00:26:56,800 --> 00:26:59,280
This is good, on a good track.

516
00:26:59,280 --> 00:26:59,780
Yeah.

517
00:26:59,780 --> 00:27:00,620
10.

518
00:27:00,620 --> 00:27:02,460
10?

519
00:27:02,460 --> 00:27:03,140
Died.

520
00:27:03,140 --> 00:27:04,620
OK.

521
00:27:04,620 --> 00:27:06,240
Yeah?

522
00:27:06,240 --> 00:27:06,740
Seven.

523
00:27:06,740 --> 00:27:08,100
Seven?

524
00:27:08,100 --> 00:27:08,600
Lived.

525
00:27:08,600 --> 00:27:09,230
OK.

526
00:27:09,230 --> 00:27:12,590
So one, four and seven
are looking better than 10

527
00:27:12,590 --> 00:27:14,230
right now.

528
00:27:14,230 --> 00:27:16,290
Someone else?

529
00:27:16,290 --> 00:27:16,790
Yeah?

530
00:27:16,790 --> 00:27:17,630
Six?

531
00:27:17,630 --> 00:27:18,330
Six?

532
00:27:18,330 --> 00:27:19,330
Still exploring.

533
00:27:19,330 --> 00:27:22,030
Patient died.

534
00:27:22,030 --> 00:27:24,230
Five?

535
00:27:24,230 --> 00:27:24,810
Died.

536
00:27:24,810 --> 00:27:27,790
OK, not so good.

537
00:27:27,790 --> 00:27:28,930
Who else?

538
00:27:28,930 --> 00:27:29,430
Yeah.

539
00:27:29,430 --> 00:27:30,430
Seven again.

540
00:27:30,430 --> 00:27:30,930
Seven.

541
00:27:30,930 --> 00:27:32,513
OK, maybe we want
to start exploiting.

542
00:27:32,513 --> 00:27:35,430
Patient lived again, so
that seems pretty good.

543
00:27:35,430 --> 00:27:37,270
Who wants to suggest?

544
00:27:37,270 --> 00:27:38,430
Yeah.

545
00:27:38,430 --> 00:27:39,870
Two.

546
00:27:39,870 --> 00:27:41,950
Patient died.

547
00:27:41,950 --> 00:27:45,190
Not so good.

548
00:27:45,190 --> 00:27:45,760
Yeah.

549
00:27:45,760 --> 00:27:46,260
Seven.

550
00:27:46,260 --> 00:27:48,410
OK.

551
00:27:48,410 --> 00:27:49,790
Patient lived again.

552
00:27:49,790 --> 00:27:52,150
Pretty good.

553
00:27:52,150 --> 00:27:54,850
What do we want to do next?

554
00:27:54,850 --> 00:27:55,350
Seven?

555
00:27:55,350 --> 00:27:58,270
OK, do we want to
keep doing seven?

556
00:27:58,270 --> 00:28:00,550
Patient died.

557
00:28:00,550 --> 00:28:01,670
Yeah?

558
00:28:01,670 --> 00:28:02,790
Eight?

559
00:28:02,790 --> 00:28:05,262
Patient died.

560
00:28:05,262 --> 00:28:06,270
Seven?

561
00:28:06,270 --> 00:28:07,340
Patient lived.

562
00:28:07,340 --> 00:28:09,840
All right, should we just keep
on going seven at this point?

563
00:28:09,840 --> 00:28:11,960
Are we happy with seven?

564
00:28:11,960 --> 00:28:13,600
Try nine.

565
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:16,160
Patient lived.

566
00:28:16,160 --> 00:28:17,000
Try one.

567
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:18,400
Try one.

568
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:20,720
Patient died.

569
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:23,480
Four, patient lived.

570
00:28:23,480 --> 00:28:24,840
Four again.

571
00:28:24,840 --> 00:28:25,720
Patient died.

572
00:28:25,720 --> 00:28:29,080

573
00:28:29,080 --> 00:28:32,360
Nine, patient lived.

574
00:28:32,360 --> 00:28:35,480
Three, patient died.

575
00:28:35,480 --> 00:28:37,872
Nine, patient lived.

576
00:28:37,872 --> 00:28:40,540
OK, we like nine?

577
00:28:40,540 --> 00:28:41,040
Cool.

578
00:28:41,040 --> 00:28:44,840
Maybe let's keep on doing nine.

579
00:28:44,840 --> 00:28:46,140
Patient is living a lot.

580
00:28:46,140 --> 00:28:48,620
Great.

581
00:28:48,620 --> 00:28:50,440
We have one patient died.

582
00:28:50,440 --> 00:28:55,640
So let's say that we kept on
going with nine for a while.

583
00:28:55,640 --> 00:28:57,320
And then we can plot--

584
00:28:57,320 --> 00:29:00,440
actually, well, with 5,000,
which I could just say keep

585
00:29:00,440 --> 00:29:01,560
on doing nine.

586
00:29:01,560 --> 00:29:04,300
Maybe we'll stop there and we
can actually plot the regret.

587
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:10,025
So this was the true
efficacy of the drugs,

588
00:29:10,025 --> 00:29:12,150
and we can see that nine
actually was the best one,

589
00:29:12,150 --> 00:29:15,650
so we actually found the
best one, which is good.

590
00:29:15,650 --> 00:29:17,627
Seven was also pretty good.

591
00:29:17,627 --> 00:29:19,210
And then we could
also plot the number

592
00:29:19,210 --> 00:29:21,910
of lives that are saved
by different algorithms.

593
00:29:21,910 --> 00:29:23,930
And so higher here is better.

594
00:29:23,930 --> 00:29:25,997
And we see that the
posterior sampling approach--

595
00:29:25,997 --> 00:29:28,330
And in this case, what posterior
sampling would be doing

596
00:29:28,330 --> 00:29:31,690
is modeling the probability
of the efficacy of each drug.

597
00:29:31,690 --> 00:29:33,670
And then based on
this estimate of that,

598
00:29:33,670 --> 00:29:37,010
picking something that's better.

599
00:29:37,010 --> 00:29:39,510
And we can see that how well
we did right here is in purple.

600
00:29:39,510 --> 00:29:41,510
It's a little bit hard
to tell just because it's

601
00:29:41,510 --> 00:29:43,610
a little small, but we can
see that, generally, it

602
00:29:43,610 --> 00:29:48,930
looks like we're doing pretty
well relative to the algorithms.

603
00:29:48,930 --> 00:29:51,467
And then you can also
plot regret over time.

604
00:29:51,467 --> 00:29:54,050
And actually, here we can see
that we're actually doing better

605
00:29:54,050 --> 00:29:54,910
than all the algorithms.

606
00:29:54,910 --> 00:29:56,077
So you guys are pretty good.

607
00:29:56,077 --> 00:29:57,605
Nice.

608
00:29:57,605 --> 00:29:59,230
Maybe there's some
luck involved there,

609
00:29:59,230 --> 00:30:00,845
so this is just a single run.

610
00:30:00,845 --> 00:30:03,220
And what these different
algorithms are is you see greedy

611
00:30:03,220 --> 00:30:04,300
is showing green.

612
00:30:04,300 --> 00:30:07,020
Greedy is just picking the
best thing you've seen so far.

613
00:30:07,020 --> 00:30:09,545
And over time, especially
with more and more trials

614
00:30:09,545 --> 00:30:11,420
that you might be running,
like more and more

615
00:30:11,420 --> 00:30:13,003
patients, more and
more ad placements,

616
00:30:13,003 --> 00:30:15,492
whatever, it's
getting a lot worse.

617
00:30:15,492 --> 00:30:17,700
In this case, we saw that
posterior sampling, yellow,

618
00:30:17,700 --> 00:30:20,660
is by far the best algorithm.

619
00:30:20,660 --> 00:30:24,620
And then the other algorithms,
red is showing epsilon greedy.

620
00:30:24,620 --> 00:30:26,820
Blue is showing UCB, so
that's the first algorithm

621
00:30:26,820 --> 00:30:28,580
we talked about with
being optimistic

622
00:30:28,580 --> 00:30:30,380
about uncertain actions.

623
00:30:30,380 --> 00:30:32,580
So this is basically
showing the trade-off

624
00:30:32,580 --> 00:30:34,627
between different algorithms.

625
00:30:34,627 --> 00:30:36,460
One other thing to note
that epsilon greedy,

626
00:30:36,460 --> 00:30:37,900
it's a bit more
greedy, and so we

627
00:30:37,900 --> 00:30:39,775
see that with-- when
you have fewer patients,

628
00:30:39,775 --> 00:30:41,720
it actually is doing
better than UCB.

629
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:43,780
But then in the long
run, UCB is doing

630
00:30:43,780 --> 00:30:47,780
better because it's actually
optimizing a bit more

631
00:30:47,780 --> 00:30:50,800
for making sure that you're
exploring more actions.

632
00:30:50,800 --> 00:30:52,860
And so in the long run,
it's able to take more

633
00:30:52,860 --> 00:30:55,700
optimal actions in the future.

634
00:30:55,700 --> 00:30:57,700
Is there a
mathematical reason why

635
00:30:57,700 --> 00:31:04,190
posterior sampling does better
than UCB and epsilon greedy?

636
00:31:04,190 --> 00:31:04,690
Yeah.

637
00:31:04,690 --> 00:31:07,910
So I don't want to go
too much into the math.

638
00:31:07,910 --> 00:31:11,030
And actually, posterior
sampling, for this MDP,

639
00:31:11,030 --> 00:31:12,950
you probably could analyze it.

640
00:31:12,950 --> 00:31:15,710

641
00:31:15,710 --> 00:31:18,630
But in general, it
can be hard to derive

642
00:31:18,630 --> 00:31:21,310
general theoretical
guarantees for it.

643
00:31:21,310 --> 00:31:29,190
With regard to epsilon
greedy, I guess some of it

644
00:31:29,190 --> 00:31:31,550
comes down to that
value of epsilon,

645
00:31:31,550 --> 00:31:34,030
and then tuning it to be--

646
00:31:34,030 --> 00:31:36,190
and generally, with
epsilon greedy,

647
00:31:36,190 --> 00:31:41,070
you often choose epsilon to be
smaller and smaller over time.

648
00:31:41,070 --> 00:31:44,870
And that hyperparameter would
probably drastically impact

649
00:31:44,870 --> 00:31:47,750
performance, because at
this point in training,

650
00:31:47,750 --> 00:31:50,590
if epsilon is constant, you're
going to be keeping on--

651
00:31:50,590 --> 00:31:52,150
you're going to be
still occasionally

652
00:31:52,150 --> 00:31:53,635
taking random drugs.

653
00:31:53,635 --> 00:31:56,010
And at that point, you already
know a lot about the drug,

654
00:31:56,010 --> 00:31:58,480
and prescribing a random
drug is a very poor choice.

655
00:31:58,480 --> 00:32:01,330

656
00:32:01,330 --> 00:32:02,170
Cool.

657
00:32:02,170 --> 00:32:05,450
So also, if you want
to play more, you can--

658
00:32:05,450 --> 00:32:09,050
I'll put the website
in the slides.

659
00:32:09,050 --> 00:32:12,170
So that's exploration
in bandits.

660
00:32:12,170 --> 00:32:15,490
Now, what does this look like
for robotics, language models

661
00:32:15,490 --> 00:32:17,690
and larger MDPs?

662
00:32:17,690 --> 00:32:19,770
Different people have
different opinions on this.

663
00:32:19,770 --> 00:32:23,850
I have a somewhat
pessimistic opinion on it.

664
00:32:23,850 --> 00:32:25,490
And in particular,
there's actually

665
00:32:25,490 --> 00:32:30,170
a lot of research that try to
translate these ideas to larger

666
00:32:30,170 --> 00:32:31,410
MDPs.

667
00:32:31,410 --> 00:32:34,330
For example, you
can try to formulate

668
00:32:34,330 --> 00:32:38,210
some estimate of whether
you've taken an action or not,

669
00:32:38,210 --> 00:32:40,910
and give reward
bonuses for novelty,

670
00:32:40,910 --> 00:32:44,650
for visiting new states,
for taking new actions.

671
00:32:44,650 --> 00:32:47,230
And similarly, like
posterior sampling,

672
00:32:47,230 --> 00:32:51,210
you can try to figure out
models of different dynamics

673
00:32:51,210 --> 00:32:54,130
and so forth and
act accordingly.

674
00:32:54,130 --> 00:32:57,380
But at the end of the day, when
you're in a very large MDP,

675
00:32:57,380 --> 00:33:01,060
exploration from scratch
is generally intractable.

676
00:33:01,060 --> 00:33:03,660
So for example, if you randomly
initialize a transformer

677
00:33:03,660 --> 00:33:07,580
language model and
try to figure out

678
00:33:07,580 --> 00:33:12,780
how to make a user happy, or
some other reward function,

679
00:33:12,780 --> 00:33:15,300
like upvotes or
something like that,

680
00:33:15,300 --> 00:33:18,140
actually, just using
upvotes to train a language

681
00:33:18,140 --> 00:33:21,420
model from scratch
isn't going to work.

682
00:33:21,420 --> 00:33:26,100
The sheer vastness of the
possible space of tokens

683
00:33:26,100 --> 00:33:30,260
is way too large, at least
relative to the patients

684
00:33:30,260 --> 00:33:33,060
that most users will have.

685
00:33:33,060 --> 00:33:34,900
Likewise, in robotics,
if you're trying

686
00:33:34,900 --> 00:33:37,380
to even just learn how to--

687
00:33:37,380 --> 00:33:41,580
I don't know-- pour water
from one cup to another cup,

688
00:33:41,580 --> 00:33:44,300
you're going to be stuck in a
realm where you're just randomly

689
00:33:44,300 --> 00:33:46,580
moving arms around,
rather than actually

690
00:33:46,580 --> 00:33:49,260
doing things that are useful
if you're starting completely

691
00:33:49,260 --> 00:33:49,810
from scratch.

692
00:33:49,810 --> 00:33:52,540

693
00:33:52,540 --> 00:33:56,110
Instead, generally,
what exploration

694
00:33:56,110 --> 00:34:00,590
looks like in these larger
domains is to rely heavily

695
00:34:00,590 --> 00:34:05,190
on demonstrations, or on base
models that were pre-trained

696
00:34:05,190 --> 00:34:07,570
on data and demonstrations.

697
00:34:07,570 --> 00:34:09,710
So for example,
reinforcement learning

698
00:34:09,710 --> 00:34:11,989
has been quite successful
in language models,

699
00:34:11,989 --> 00:34:14,310
because, and
particularly in scenarios

700
00:34:14,310 --> 00:34:16,469
where you have a strong
base model that already

701
00:34:16,469 --> 00:34:18,389
knows how to write
coherent sentences,

702
00:34:18,389 --> 00:34:21,190
and already knows
roughly how to approach

703
00:34:21,190 --> 00:34:24,389
certain kinds of prompts, or
certain kinds of problems.

704
00:34:24,389 --> 00:34:28,429
And likewise, in robotics,
if you have demonstrations

705
00:34:28,429 --> 00:34:31,070
of example behavior that
achieve high reward,

706
00:34:31,070 --> 00:34:35,870
then using that as a starting
point for reinforcement learning

707
00:34:35,870 --> 00:34:36,619
is very powerful.

708
00:34:36,619 --> 00:34:39,429

709
00:34:39,429 --> 00:34:42,550
And generally, these models
are providing an initialization

710
00:34:42,550 --> 00:34:44,750
that's really excellent
for exploration

711
00:34:44,750 --> 00:34:48,350
and seeking out
potential things.

712
00:34:48,350 --> 00:34:50,830
Wherever feasible,
it's also quite useful

713
00:34:50,830 --> 00:34:52,870
to use shaped
rewards, because that

714
00:34:52,870 --> 00:34:56,280
can guide any sort of
exploration process, as well.

715
00:34:56,280 --> 00:34:58,780
And this could be if
you're in simulations.

716
00:34:58,780 --> 00:35:01,860
You can hand code
sparse, dense rewards,

717
00:35:01,860 --> 00:35:04,760
or if you're learning
single-step rewards

718
00:35:04,760 --> 00:35:07,120
from preferences, that
actually also provides

719
00:35:07,120 --> 00:35:09,760
a fairly dense reward signal.

720
00:35:09,760 --> 00:35:12,760
This isn't to say you could
also combine some of these ideas

721
00:35:12,760 --> 00:35:15,760
with demonstrations
and base models.

722
00:35:15,760 --> 00:35:19,520
But generally, you
get a lot further

723
00:35:19,520 --> 00:35:24,280
from starting with this
kind of initialization.

724
00:35:24,280 --> 00:35:27,780
That said, there is still
potentially an exploration

725
00:35:27,780 --> 00:35:29,780
problem that we started
to talk about last time.

726
00:35:29,780 --> 00:35:32,800
And I want to revisit that,
now that we've actually

727
00:35:32,800 --> 00:35:39,040
started to learn about
exploration in standard MDPs.

728
00:35:39,040 --> 00:35:39,940
Yeah, exactly.

729
00:35:39,940 --> 00:35:44,700
So the idea here is that if
you're in these kinds of MDPs,

730
00:35:44,700 --> 00:35:48,560
the state space is enormous, and
all of the possible actions you

731
00:35:48,560 --> 00:35:50,640
can take is enormous, as well.

732
00:35:50,640 --> 00:35:54,290
And this is a way
to bias the model

733
00:35:54,290 --> 00:35:56,010
towards a part of
the state space

734
00:35:56,010 --> 00:36:01,040
that is going to
get high reward.

735
00:36:01,040 --> 00:36:03,290
Yeah, giving it bias of,
these are the kinds of things

736
00:36:03,290 --> 00:36:05,836
I want you to do.

737
00:36:05,836 --> 00:36:08,730
So in particular, what
I'd like to talk about is,

738
00:36:08,730 --> 00:36:12,010
say you have some model
that has been trained,

739
00:36:12,010 --> 00:36:13,470
and it can do a
variety of things.

740
00:36:13,470 --> 00:36:15,770
But there's scenarios
where when you're actually

741
00:36:15,770 --> 00:36:18,770
deploying that
policy, you actually,

742
00:36:18,770 --> 00:36:20,830
even if you have a lot
of prior experience,

743
00:36:20,830 --> 00:36:23,887
you might need to
explore at test time

744
00:36:23,887 --> 00:36:25,970
because you might not have
all the information you

745
00:36:25,970 --> 00:36:28,370
need to get high reward.

746
00:36:28,370 --> 00:36:31,170
And so we talked about a
couple examples on Wednesday

747
00:36:31,170 --> 00:36:33,930
where, for example, you might
need to find ingredients

748
00:36:33,930 --> 00:36:34,870
in a kitchen.

749
00:36:34,870 --> 00:36:36,910
And to do that, you
can't just start cooking.

750
00:36:36,910 --> 00:36:38,890
You need to open
drawers, open cabinets,

751
00:36:38,890 --> 00:36:42,570
and so forth to try to
seek out information.

752
00:36:42,570 --> 00:36:46,445
And second, in language
models for reasoning,

753
00:36:46,445 --> 00:36:48,570
you might need to explore
different strategies that

754
00:36:48,570 --> 00:36:52,780
might lead to a
solution to a problem,

755
00:36:52,780 --> 00:36:56,460
rather than simply trying to
start writing tokens that you

756
00:36:56,460 --> 00:36:59,980
think might lead
to the solution.

757
00:36:59,980 --> 00:37:02,420
Now, we talked about this
kind of meta-RL problem

758
00:37:02,420 --> 00:37:07,540
where we're optimizing a policy
with history, or with memory.

759
00:37:07,540 --> 00:37:10,020
And generally,
memory should allow

760
00:37:10,020 --> 00:37:13,140
agents to be able to
explore and exploit,

761
00:37:13,140 --> 00:37:15,660
because they're able to
try different actions,

762
00:37:15,660 --> 00:37:17,553
and remember what
they did, and then use

763
00:37:17,553 --> 00:37:19,220
that information about
where ingredients

764
00:37:19,220 --> 00:37:21,740
are to then solve the problem.

765
00:37:21,740 --> 00:37:24,020
But we talked about how
if we optimize exploration

766
00:37:24,020 --> 00:37:27,100
and exploitation together
with an end-to-end objective,

767
00:37:27,100 --> 00:37:27,900
this is hard.

768
00:37:27,900 --> 00:37:29,400
Because we have
this chicken and egg

769
00:37:29,400 --> 00:37:33,060
problem, where if we haven't
yet learned how to explore, then

770
00:37:33,060 --> 00:37:35,820
we can't actually get
high rewards because we

771
00:37:35,820 --> 00:37:37,460
haven't found the ingredients.

772
00:37:37,460 --> 00:37:40,000
And likewise, if we don't yet
know how to solve the task,

773
00:37:40,000 --> 00:37:45,260
then we get low reward for any
sort of exploration behavior.

774
00:37:45,260 --> 00:37:48,430
So there's this
coupling problem.

775
00:37:48,430 --> 00:37:51,550
And we also talked about
some alternative exploration

776
00:37:51,550 --> 00:37:54,030
strategies that are
easier to optimize,

777
00:37:54,030 --> 00:37:58,890
but can lead to
suboptimal performance.

778
00:37:58,890 --> 00:38:02,510
And what I'd like
to talk about is,

779
00:38:02,510 --> 00:38:06,510
there's actually a method that
can both get optimal exploration

780
00:38:06,510 --> 00:38:09,870
and exploitation trade-off,
and also be efficient to train.

781
00:38:09,870 --> 00:38:12,775
So it sort of gets the
best of both worlds.

782
00:38:12,775 --> 00:38:14,150
And the idea for
this is actually

783
00:38:14,150 --> 00:38:17,950
going to build upon this
last alternative exploration

784
00:38:17,950 --> 00:38:19,870
strategy.

785
00:38:19,870 --> 00:38:23,670
Let's write this right here.

786
00:38:23,670 --> 00:38:30,150
So the idea here is
that you can try to--

787
00:38:30,150 --> 00:38:32,210
for different environments
or different tasks,

788
00:38:32,210 --> 00:38:36,710
you could try to fit a dynamics
model and a reward model,

789
00:38:36,710 --> 00:38:41,310
and then try to explore
in ways that tell us

790
00:38:41,310 --> 00:38:45,190
more information about our
model of the environment.

791
00:38:45,190 --> 00:38:50,120
And this should incentivize
the agent or the policy

792
00:38:50,120 --> 00:38:52,040
to actually seek out
additional information

793
00:38:52,040 --> 00:38:54,120
to explore in a way
that's decoupled

794
00:38:54,120 --> 00:39:01,360
from executing on the task.

795
00:39:01,360 --> 00:39:05,880
And so if you do this and
you have different MDPs that

796
00:39:05,880 --> 00:39:08,960
correspond to navigating
to different circles,

797
00:39:08,960 --> 00:39:12,720
and then you try to incentivize
it to take trajectories that

798
00:39:12,720 --> 00:39:14,160
lead to a better
model of reward,

799
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:16,200
and better model of
dynamics, you actually

800
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:17,838
get a trajectory
that looks something

801
00:39:17,838 --> 00:39:19,880
like the purple trajectory,
where it's explicitly

802
00:39:19,880 --> 00:39:21,255
trying to visit
states that allow

803
00:39:21,255 --> 00:39:26,920
it to learn where the reward
function is going to be.

804
00:39:26,920 --> 00:39:30,500
Now, this can learn efficient
exploration strategies,

805
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:32,640
but it has its
downsides and it can be

806
00:39:32,640 --> 00:39:35,320
suboptimal in some scenarios.

807
00:39:35,320 --> 00:39:36,910
For example, if
there's distractors,

808
00:39:36,910 --> 00:39:40,098
like if there's pictures
on the wall, for example,

809
00:39:40,098 --> 00:39:42,640
then it's going to be looking
at all the pictures on the wall

810
00:39:42,640 --> 00:39:46,030
to learn a good model of
those different environments.

811
00:39:46,030 --> 00:39:49,170

812
00:39:49,170 --> 00:39:52,810
So we can build upon
this idea and try

813
00:39:52,810 --> 00:39:57,410
to learn how to explore
without learning a model.

814
00:39:57,410 --> 00:40:01,570
And in particular, instead
of trying to learn a dynamics

815
00:40:01,570 --> 00:40:04,410
and reward model,
we could instead

816
00:40:04,410 --> 00:40:09,530
try to be able to
predict what the task is.

817
00:40:09,530 --> 00:40:14,370
So we're going to label each
task with a identifier mu.

818
00:40:14,370 --> 00:40:17,690
This could be either like
a one-hot like an index,

819
00:40:17,690 --> 00:40:21,150
or it could be a
language description,

820
00:40:21,150 --> 00:40:26,570
or it could be some more
continuous description

821
00:40:26,570 --> 00:40:30,410
of the task, like
continuous vector.

822
00:40:30,410 --> 00:40:33,650
And then what we
could do is we could

823
00:40:33,650 --> 00:40:38,010
try to explore in a way
that allows us to predict

824
00:40:38,010 --> 00:40:40,610
what the task is at hand.

825
00:40:40,610 --> 00:40:46,780
So for example,
we'll have our say

826
00:40:46,780 --> 00:40:48,900
that we have two policies,
and exploration policy

827
00:40:48,900 --> 00:40:50,700
and exploitation policy.

828
00:40:50,700 --> 00:40:53,060
In practice you
could combine these,

829
00:40:53,060 --> 00:40:59,000
but it's helpful to think
about them as separate.

830
00:40:59,000 --> 00:41:02,960
So we'll have our exploration
strategy or exploration policy.

831
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:09,300
This is going to produce some
experience in a particular task.

832
00:41:09,300 --> 00:41:11,640
And our goal is for it
to be able to explore,

833
00:41:11,640 --> 00:41:13,780
to be able to discern
different tasks.

834
00:41:13,780 --> 00:41:17,340
And so we could
separately train a model

835
00:41:17,340 --> 00:41:20,180
that's going to be
predicting the task

836
00:41:20,180 --> 00:41:26,100
identifier from the experience
that we've collected so far.

837
00:41:26,100 --> 00:41:28,620
And if we do
something like this,

838
00:41:28,620 --> 00:41:32,060
then this sort of
exploration objective

839
00:41:32,060 --> 00:41:36,300
is completely separate from
actually executing on the task.

840
00:41:36,300 --> 00:41:40,800
The goal here is to be able to
predict the task identifier.

841
00:41:40,800 --> 00:41:42,760
And then separate
from this, we could

842
00:41:42,760 --> 00:41:48,960
train a policy that's trying
to execute on the task,

843
00:41:48,960 --> 00:41:53,480
just using the
identifier for the task.

844
00:41:53,480 --> 00:41:56,040
And this is just
multi-task learning

845
00:41:56,040 --> 00:41:57,660
or multi-task
reinforcement learning,

846
00:41:57,660 --> 00:42:02,340
and this we can train just
with optimizing reward.

847
00:42:02,340 --> 00:42:06,200
And it doesn't rely on
having good exploration.

848
00:42:06,200 --> 00:42:09,820
So here, we're able to decouple
the objective for execution

849
00:42:09,820 --> 00:42:11,400
so it doesn't rely
on exploration,

850
00:42:11,400 --> 00:42:14,600
and have an exploration
objective just based

851
00:42:14,600 --> 00:42:19,320
on identifying the task that
isn't dependent on how well you

852
00:42:19,320 --> 00:42:21,535
do the task.

853
00:42:21,535 --> 00:42:24,160
So on the slides, we'll train a
policy in a task identification

854
00:42:24,160 --> 00:42:28,360
model so that we collect data
that allows for accurate task

855
00:42:28,360 --> 00:42:29,840
prediction.

856
00:42:29,840 --> 00:42:31,320
And then we'll
also train a policy

857
00:42:31,320 --> 00:42:35,600
that's conditioned
on the task ID,

858
00:42:35,600 --> 00:42:40,442
in order to maximize
reward for each task.

859
00:42:40,442 --> 00:42:42,150
So which part of this
is the exploration?

860
00:42:42,150 --> 00:42:44,650
Is it the model that
predicts the identifier?

861
00:42:44,650 --> 00:42:47,210
So the exploration is really
this policy right here.

862
00:42:47,210 --> 00:42:50,090
And this model is
actually just going

863
00:42:50,090 --> 00:42:52,230
to be used for the
reward for this.

864
00:42:52,230 --> 00:42:54,650
And so we're going to be--

865
00:42:54,650 --> 00:42:57,290
the reward for this
policy will be, basically,

866
00:42:57,290 --> 00:43:01,610
how well this model is
identifying the task,

867
00:43:01,610 --> 00:43:07,490
or basically, the negative error
of this task identifier model.

868
00:43:07,490 --> 00:43:08,230
Good question.

869
00:43:08,230 --> 00:43:10,050
The question is,
does this generalize

870
00:43:10,050 --> 00:43:12,130
to new unseen task categories?

871
00:43:12,130 --> 00:43:16,850
This can actually struggle
with generalization,

872
00:43:16,850 --> 00:43:18,470
and we'll talk about that.

873
00:43:18,470 --> 00:43:21,053
Let's go through meta-testing,
and then we'll talk about that.

874
00:43:21,053 --> 00:43:24,265
So this is roughly
how training works.

875
00:43:24,265 --> 00:43:25,890
And then at test time
if you've trained

876
00:43:25,890 --> 00:43:27,515
all of these different
things, then you

877
00:43:27,515 --> 00:43:28,910
can take your
exploration policy,

878
00:43:28,910 --> 00:43:33,010
run it to collect experience,
then feed that experience

879
00:43:33,010 --> 00:43:36,450
into your task identifier
model to get a task ID,

880
00:43:36,450 --> 00:43:39,380
and then take that task
ID, and feed that as input

881
00:43:39,380 --> 00:43:43,120
to your execution policy
to then execute the task.

882
00:43:43,120 --> 00:43:46,392
So you'll then explore and
then identify the task,

883
00:43:46,392 --> 00:43:47,100
and then exploit.

884
00:43:47,100 --> 00:43:49,740

885
00:43:49,740 --> 00:43:53,980
Now, getting back to that
question, the upside of this

886
00:43:53,980 --> 00:43:56,320
is you don't need to
model dynamics or rewards.

887
00:43:56,320 --> 00:43:59,020
You're just learning
this model that's

888
00:43:59,020 --> 00:44:01,180
predicting the task identifier.

889
00:44:01,180 --> 00:44:03,340
But if the task
identifier is just

890
00:44:03,340 --> 00:44:06,580
a one-hot vector, for
example, this probably

891
00:44:06,580 --> 00:44:10,060
isn't going to generalize
to new tasks very well.

892
00:44:10,060 --> 00:44:13,387
And we'll see a solution
to that on the next slide.

893
00:44:13,387 --> 00:44:15,220
The question is, what
is the main difference

894
00:44:15,220 --> 00:44:19,420
from black box meta-RL that
we talked about before?

895
00:44:19,420 --> 00:44:23,100
So we are still
sampling different MDPs.

896
00:44:23,100 --> 00:44:25,700
The main difference is
simply the objective

897
00:44:25,700 --> 00:44:29,140
for the exploration part.

898
00:44:29,140 --> 00:44:31,760
So well, I guess there's
two main differences.

899
00:44:31,760 --> 00:44:34,160
One is the objective or
the exploration part,

900
00:44:34,160 --> 00:44:36,190
and then the second
is that here we're

901
00:44:36,190 --> 00:44:39,630
going to be maximizing rewards,
similar to black box meta-RL.

902
00:44:39,630 --> 00:44:42,270
But we're actually going to be
giving it this task identifier

903
00:44:42,270 --> 00:44:45,590
so that it doesn't need
to rely on exploration,

904
00:44:45,590 --> 00:44:47,470
or a good exploration
strategy when trying

905
00:44:47,470 --> 00:44:50,110
to learn how to do the task.

906
00:44:50,110 --> 00:44:52,830
Now we have this
generalization problem,

907
00:44:52,830 --> 00:44:55,370
and we actually also have
a way to address this.

908
00:44:55,370 --> 00:44:59,850
So instead of using this
task identifier directly,

909
00:44:59,850 --> 00:45:03,510
we can actually try to learn a
more compressed representation

910
00:45:03,510 --> 00:45:07,270
of the task that will
generalize more effectively

911
00:45:07,270 --> 00:45:08,990
than a one-hot vector.

912
00:45:08,990 --> 00:45:13,432
So as an example, when we
learn the execution policy,

913
00:45:13,432 --> 00:45:15,390
we're also going to
identify information that's

914
00:45:15,390 --> 00:45:17,470
needed for solving the task.

915
00:45:17,470 --> 00:45:19,695
And so say that our
identifier consists

916
00:45:19,695 --> 00:45:21,070
of a few things
that are relevant

917
00:45:21,070 --> 00:45:23,110
and a few things that
are not relevant.

918
00:45:23,110 --> 00:45:24,870
So it contains things
like wall color

919
00:45:24,870 --> 00:45:26,550
and decorations,
but then it also

920
00:45:26,550 --> 00:45:30,030
contains information about
where the ingredients are.

921
00:45:30,030 --> 00:45:32,170
Then we'd like to
use-- and similar

922
00:45:32,170 --> 00:45:33,920
if you had a one-hot
vector, it would also

923
00:45:33,920 --> 00:45:35,820
contain information that
might be irrelevant,

924
00:45:35,820 --> 00:45:38,195
because you might have two
Kitchens that have ingredients

925
00:45:38,195 --> 00:45:41,280
in the same place, but then have
other things that are spurious.

926
00:45:41,280 --> 00:45:43,640
And we'll try to then
bottleneck this representation

927
00:45:43,640 --> 00:45:45,840
to compress away
the stuff that's not

928
00:45:45,840 --> 00:45:50,440
needed for solving the task.

929
00:45:50,440 --> 00:45:53,080
And if we can do this, then
this sort of representation

930
00:45:53,080 --> 00:45:55,560
should generalize better.

931
00:45:55,560 --> 00:45:58,420
And then when we
learn to explore,

932
00:45:58,420 --> 00:46:00,640
instead of learning
to explore to be

933
00:46:00,640 --> 00:46:02,345
able to predict the
identifier here,

934
00:46:02,345 --> 00:46:03,720
we'll learn to
explore so that we

935
00:46:03,720 --> 00:46:08,800
can recover this compressed
task representation.

936
00:46:08,800 --> 00:46:12,400
And so try to be able to
explore so that you're

937
00:46:12,400 --> 00:46:17,340
able to predict what this z
representation for the task is.

938
00:46:17,340 --> 00:46:19,880

939
00:46:19,880 --> 00:46:23,360
Now, how do you actually
compress representations

940
00:46:23,360 --> 00:46:24,295
in neural networks?

941
00:46:24,295 --> 00:46:26,420
So before we go into the
details of this algorithm,

942
00:46:26,420 --> 00:46:29,320
let's talk about
that a little bit.

943
00:46:29,320 --> 00:46:39,610
So we have our task
identifier mu, mu i,

944
00:46:39,610 --> 00:46:44,210
and then we'd like to
have a neural network that

945
00:46:44,210 --> 00:46:49,770
uses that to predict zi.

946
00:46:49,770 --> 00:46:51,650
And then, we'll
then have a policy

947
00:46:51,650 --> 00:46:58,770
that takes this and the
state and outputs the action.

948
00:46:58,770 --> 00:47:04,316
And if we train this whole
thing end to end, it may not--

949
00:47:04,316 --> 00:47:06,130
it's not necessarily
incentivized

950
00:47:06,130 --> 00:47:08,890
to actually compress
information here,

951
00:47:08,890 --> 00:47:13,610
to actually remove information
about decorations, for example.

952
00:47:13,610 --> 00:47:18,490
So one thing we could do is
we could simply add noise

953
00:47:18,490 --> 00:47:21,370
to this representation.

954
00:47:21,370 --> 00:47:24,810
And so if you take
this, and instead

955
00:47:24,810 --> 00:47:30,700
of feeding it directly here,
you could take this output zi.

956
00:47:30,700 --> 00:47:34,920
And then right here, you could
basically add some noise.

957
00:47:34,920 --> 00:47:36,940
So right here, what
you're doing is

958
00:47:36,940 --> 00:47:41,820
you're adding some noise
where the noise is sampled

959
00:47:41,820 --> 00:47:45,140
from a Gaussian distribution.

960
00:47:45,140 --> 00:47:49,460
And if you add noise, then this
is going to discard information,

961
00:47:49,460 --> 00:47:51,300
because you're adding--

962
00:47:51,300 --> 00:47:54,700
you're kind of
corrupting the signal.

963
00:47:54,700 --> 00:47:58,660
But you might discard
some good information,

964
00:47:58,660 --> 00:48:01,517
especially if you do
this only at test time.

965
00:48:01,517 --> 00:48:03,100
Now in principle,
you could also train

966
00:48:03,100 --> 00:48:08,700
with this noise to encourage it
to discard information from z.

967
00:48:08,700 --> 00:48:12,860
But if you train the policy
with this noise as input,

968
00:48:12,860 --> 00:48:15,860
then what it could just
do is it could just

969
00:48:15,860 --> 00:48:18,020
increase the
magnitude of zi here,

970
00:48:18,020 --> 00:48:19,460
so that the magnitude
of the noise

971
00:48:19,460 --> 00:48:22,555
is a lot smaller in comparison.

972
00:48:22,555 --> 00:48:24,180
And so if you just
add noise, the model

973
00:48:24,180 --> 00:48:27,830
can compensate and still
pass a lot of information

974
00:48:27,830 --> 00:48:31,430
through this task
representation.

975
00:48:31,430 --> 00:48:36,768
So that's not a great
solution, but what we can do

976
00:48:36,768 --> 00:48:38,310
is we can just also
prevent the model

977
00:48:38,310 --> 00:48:40,470
from increasing the magnitude.

978
00:48:40,470 --> 00:48:44,750
And we can just
add a loss that--

979
00:48:44,750 --> 00:48:47,590
we can modify our training
loss for the policy

980
00:48:47,590 --> 00:48:55,710
to include a term that's going
to minimize the magnitude of z.

981
00:48:55,710 --> 00:48:58,350
And if you do
this, you're then--

982
00:48:58,350 --> 00:49:00,870
now if it kind of blows
up the magnitude of z,

983
00:49:00,870 --> 00:49:04,930
then it will have
a high loss here.

984
00:49:04,930 --> 00:49:07,150
And so it will be forced
to keep that magnitude down

985
00:49:07,150 --> 00:49:11,430
to be lower, so that it is
actually forced to remove

986
00:49:11,430 --> 00:49:14,230
information that isn't needed.

987
00:49:14,230 --> 00:49:16,607
And any sort of magnitude
it does introduce,

988
00:49:16,607 --> 00:49:19,190
it will be incentivized to only
have information that's needed

989
00:49:19,190 --> 00:49:22,950
for predicting the action.

990
00:49:22,950 --> 00:49:27,000
This is called a variational
information bottleneck, which

991
00:49:27,000 --> 00:49:30,120
is a fancy term for saying
that you're adding noise,

992
00:49:30,120 --> 00:49:32,200
and then also
modifying your loss

993
00:49:32,200 --> 00:49:38,175
term to have this L2 penalty
on your representation.

994
00:49:38,175 --> 00:49:40,800
One of the reasons why this is
called a variational information

995
00:49:40,800 --> 00:49:44,480
bottleneck is that this
objective is actually

996
00:49:44,480 --> 00:49:51,800
equivalent to minimizing the
KL divergence between your--

997
00:49:51,800 --> 00:49:53,760
If you think about
this as a distribution,

998
00:49:53,760 --> 00:49:55,885
because this is now kind
of a Gaussian distribution

999
00:49:55,885 --> 00:49:58,360
with a mean of zi and
a variance according

1000
00:49:58,360 --> 00:50:01,970
to the variance
of epsilon, that's

1001
00:50:01,970 --> 00:50:03,720
equivalent to minimizing
the KL divergence

1002
00:50:03,720 --> 00:50:09,500
between that distribution and
the unit Gaussian distribution.

1003
00:50:09,500 --> 00:50:11,000
Yeah, so the question
is, why are we

1004
00:50:11,000 --> 00:50:14,820
creating a representation
of a one-hot vector?

1005
00:50:14,820 --> 00:50:17,200
So if we go back
to the algorithm

1006
00:50:17,200 --> 00:50:19,800
we talked about
before, where we are

1007
00:50:19,800 --> 00:50:22,300
exploring to
generate experience,

1008
00:50:22,300 --> 00:50:24,500
then trying to identify
the task, and then

1009
00:50:24,500 --> 00:50:29,140
condition on that task, if
we then want to quickly solve

1010
00:50:29,140 --> 00:50:34,340
a new task, then we'll be able
to use our policy to explore.

1011
00:50:34,340 --> 00:50:39,060
But then after we explore,
if our representation is

1012
00:50:39,060 --> 00:50:41,720
a one-hot vector here,
if it's a new task,

1013
00:50:41,720 --> 00:50:43,900
then that new task
may not cleanly

1014
00:50:43,900 --> 00:50:46,680
fit within one of the
previous one-hot vectors.

1015
00:50:46,680 --> 00:50:49,380
And so we'd like to have a
more continuous space that's

1016
00:50:49,380 --> 00:50:53,140
more compactly
representing the task,

1017
00:50:53,140 --> 00:50:56,540
and essentially learn this
latent task representation that

1018
00:50:56,540 --> 00:50:58,923
will generalize to new tasks.

1019
00:50:58,923 --> 00:51:00,340
Yeah, so the
question is, don't we

1020
00:51:00,340 --> 00:51:02,100
need more information
about the task,

1021
00:51:02,100 --> 00:51:05,460
whether or not two tasks
are related to each other?

1022
00:51:05,460 --> 00:51:09,500
Though, I guess the--

1023
00:51:09,500 --> 00:51:11,660
so when you think
about a one-hot,

1024
00:51:11,660 --> 00:51:14,860
it doesn't really seem like it
is necessarily capturing that

1025
00:51:14,860 --> 00:51:19,220
much information, but a
one-hot vector is actually less

1026
00:51:19,220 --> 00:51:22,450
compressed than
something that is--

1027
00:51:22,450 --> 00:51:27,030

1028
00:51:27,030 --> 00:51:30,230
something that's a
more continuous or less

1029
00:51:30,230 --> 00:51:31,870
discrete representation.

1030
00:51:31,870 --> 00:51:35,730
So say, for example, let's see.

1031
00:51:35,730 --> 00:51:39,250
Let's say, for example, that
one of your tasks is to go here.

1032
00:51:39,250 --> 00:51:41,390
This is task one.

1033
00:51:41,390 --> 00:51:43,870
Task two is to go here.

1034
00:51:43,870 --> 00:51:49,110
Task three is to go here,
and task four is to go here.

1035
00:51:49,110 --> 00:51:51,790
Then task one and task
four are very similar,

1036
00:51:51,790 --> 00:51:56,830
but when you use one-hot
vectors to represent them,

1037
00:51:56,830 --> 00:52:02,470
they are kind of represented
completely separately.

1038
00:52:02,470 --> 00:52:08,030
And if you try to learn a
task representation that's

1039
00:52:08,030 --> 00:52:09,790
compressing these,
then it will actually

1040
00:52:09,790 --> 00:52:14,790
learn a representation that puts
these much closer to each other.

1041
00:52:14,790 --> 00:52:17,310
The question is, do you need
to add epsilon to the loss?

1042
00:52:17,310 --> 00:52:22,960
So it doesn't end up
coming into the loss,

1043
00:52:22,960 --> 00:52:25,160
but you do need to add
it when you're actually

1044
00:52:25,160 --> 00:52:26,960
training the neural network.

1045
00:52:26,960 --> 00:52:30,040
So you're adding the noise
and you sample the noise

1046
00:52:30,040 --> 00:52:33,800
every-- resample the
noise every single time.

1047
00:52:33,800 --> 00:52:36,740
And the epsilon is sampled
from this distribution,

1048
00:52:36,740 --> 00:52:38,560
and you can think
about this distribution

1049
00:52:38,560 --> 00:52:43,440
as coming into the loss.

1050
00:52:43,440 --> 00:52:47,360
But yeah, you don't need to add
epsilon anywhere into the loss.

1051
00:52:47,360 --> 00:52:49,960
It just comes out to this.

1052
00:52:49,960 --> 00:52:54,200
So let's walk through, actually,
what this algorithm looks

1053
00:52:54,200 --> 00:52:57,880
like more completely.

1054
00:52:57,880 --> 00:53:04,440
So first, let's talk about
just executing on the task

1055
00:53:04,440 --> 00:53:06,098
separate from exploration.

1056
00:53:06,098 --> 00:53:08,640
So our goal is to learn this
task representation, this latent

1057
00:53:08,640 --> 00:53:12,320
task representation, and
how to solve the task.

1058
00:53:12,320 --> 00:53:14,640
And to do this,
we're going to have--

1059
00:53:14,640 --> 00:53:16,800
we have our task identifier.

1060
00:53:16,800 --> 00:53:24,130
And we're going to have a
network that produces zi.

1061
00:53:24,130 --> 00:53:32,210
We're going to add noise
to get zi plus epsilon.

1062
00:53:32,210 --> 00:53:41,116
And then we're going to pass
this and the state into our--

1063
00:53:41,116 --> 00:53:44,090
this is our execution
policy that's

1064
00:53:44,090 --> 00:53:48,450
going to produce an action.

1065
00:53:48,450 --> 00:53:51,420
And we can think about
this as having two parts,

1066
00:53:51,420 --> 00:53:53,170
so we have the execution
policy right here

1067
00:53:53,170 --> 00:53:57,530
that takes as input the
noisy zi and the state

1068
00:53:57,530 --> 00:53:58,910
to produce the action.

1069
00:53:58,910 --> 00:54:01,770
And then we also have
this part right here.

1070
00:54:01,770 --> 00:54:08,570
And we'll write this as some
encoder F that maps from mu

1071
00:54:08,570 --> 00:54:13,090
i to zi.

1072
00:54:13,090 --> 00:54:15,690
We can write this
as a distribution,

1073
00:54:15,690 --> 00:54:22,060
because if you fold this noise
into a distribution like this,

1074
00:54:22,060 --> 00:54:27,660
this will be some
distribution that is--

1075
00:54:27,660 --> 00:54:29,940
let's now call this z bar.

1076
00:54:29,940 --> 00:54:35,060
This is a distribution that
is a Gaussian with mean

1077
00:54:35,060 --> 00:54:43,780
of z bar i and some variance.

1078
00:54:43,780 --> 00:54:47,260
Let's just write it as
variance, like that.

1079
00:54:47,260 --> 00:54:51,660
So these are the two things
that have parameters, F and pi.

1080
00:54:51,660 --> 00:54:54,660
And we're going to train
both of these neural networks

1081
00:54:54,660 --> 00:54:58,100
to maximize reward,
so that we're actually

1082
00:54:58,100 --> 00:55:01,180
learning a representation,
such that we can solve the task

1083
00:55:01,180 --> 00:55:03,780
and predict actions
that maximize reward.

1084
00:55:03,780 --> 00:55:08,980
And then we're also going to
add the regularization on z.

1085
00:55:08,980 --> 00:55:12,340
And so our objective
here will be

1086
00:55:12,340 --> 00:55:17,510
maximizing the sum
of expected rewards

1087
00:55:17,510 --> 00:55:19,700
under our execution policy.

1088
00:55:19,700 --> 00:55:25,270

1089
00:55:25,270 --> 00:55:27,630
And since we're
maximizing here, we're

1090
00:55:27,630 --> 00:55:31,910
also going to be minimizing
this term right here, which

1091
00:55:31,910 --> 00:55:34,130
is equivalent to
the KL divergence.

1092
00:55:34,130 --> 00:55:37,390
So we'll be maximizing
the negative KL

1093
00:55:37,390 --> 00:55:48,790
divergence between
F of zi, given mu i,

1094
00:55:48,790 --> 00:55:55,070
and this unit Gaussian.

1095
00:55:55,070 --> 00:55:58,370
So on the slides,
there's the objective.

1096
00:55:58,370 --> 00:56:00,330
And this is just trying
to solve the task,

1097
00:56:00,330 --> 00:56:04,230
and also learn a latent task
representation based off

1098
00:56:04,230 --> 00:56:06,590
of the task ID.

1099
00:56:06,590 --> 00:56:09,110
It's going to be a vector,
so z is going to be a vector.

1100
00:56:09,110 --> 00:56:12,175
And so this should be an i.

1101
00:56:12,175 --> 00:56:14,640
But yeah, it'll be a vector.

1102
00:56:14,640 --> 00:56:18,500
And yeah, yeah.

1103
00:56:18,500 --> 00:56:21,000
So the question is, why are
we adding this information

1104
00:56:21,000 --> 00:56:23,640
bottleneck or this
KL divergence?

1105
00:56:23,640 --> 00:56:27,280
The reason is that we
want to learn a nice task

1106
00:56:27,280 --> 00:56:29,640
representation that's
throwing away information.

1107
00:56:29,640 --> 00:56:33,980
And if we don't add that
term, then this z right here,

1108
00:56:33,980 --> 00:56:36,440
this representation of
the task might still

1109
00:56:36,440 --> 00:56:38,520
contain a lot of
information that

1110
00:56:38,520 --> 00:56:41,720
are irrelevant for
actually solving the task.

1111
00:56:41,720 --> 00:56:44,306
And so it might contain--

1112
00:56:44,306 --> 00:56:48,480
if, for example, you
have two tasks that

1113
00:56:48,480 --> 00:56:50,540
have the ingredients in
the same exact place,

1114
00:56:50,540 --> 00:56:53,160
but something has changed about
the environment, like the wall

1115
00:56:53,160 --> 00:56:55,845
color is different, or different
objects in different places,

1116
00:56:55,845 --> 00:56:57,720
but those are irrelevant
to solving the task,

1117
00:56:57,720 --> 00:57:00,380
then z will include those
things that are irrelevant.

1118
00:57:00,380 --> 00:57:02,040
And then when you go
to explore, you're

1119
00:57:02,040 --> 00:57:03,623
then going to be
exploring things that

1120
00:57:03,623 --> 00:57:06,000
are irrelevant to the task.

1121
00:57:06,000 --> 00:57:09,840
Yeah, so the question
is, we are given

1122
00:57:09,840 --> 00:57:12,730
the task ID during training.

1123
00:57:12,730 --> 00:57:15,010
We actually don't
need it at test time.

1124
00:57:15,010 --> 00:57:19,370
So what does this look like
to generalize to new tasks?

1125
00:57:19,370 --> 00:57:21,338
When you are faced
with a new task,

1126
00:57:21,338 --> 00:57:23,130
you're going to use
your exploration policy

1127
00:57:23,130 --> 00:57:26,593
to explore and then predict a--

1128
00:57:26,593 --> 00:57:28,010
instead of predicting
mu i, you're

1129
00:57:28,010 --> 00:57:31,170
actually going to predicting zi,
your latent task representation,

1130
00:57:31,170 --> 00:57:34,970
and then feeding that
into your execution policy

1131
00:57:34,970 --> 00:57:38,370
to then execute or
exploit on the task.

1132
00:57:38,370 --> 00:57:40,970
The question is, can you
train the execution policy

1133
00:57:40,970 --> 00:57:43,210
to attend to the relevant parts?

1134
00:57:43,210 --> 00:57:48,650
So if you have a really good
task representation that doesn't

1135
00:57:48,650 --> 00:57:52,250
have irrelevant parts of the--

1136
00:57:52,250 --> 00:57:57,530
irrelevant things in it,
then you can skip this step

1137
00:57:57,530 --> 00:58:00,450
and basically zero out
those parts of the task

1138
00:58:00,450 --> 00:58:01,610
representation.

1139
00:58:01,610 --> 00:58:04,318
This would be in scenarios where
you don't have a one-hot vector,

1140
00:58:04,318 --> 00:58:09,180
but you already know
maybe one task--

1141
00:58:09,180 --> 00:58:11,380
Maybe if you have language
strings, for example,

1142
00:58:11,380 --> 00:58:19,060
and one task is turn left,
one task is turn right.

1143
00:58:19,060 --> 00:58:21,560
Another task maybe is
the same as turn left.

1144
00:58:21,560 --> 00:58:23,742
It's like you have the
task string for it.

1145
00:58:23,742 --> 00:58:25,200
It's basically the
same exact task.

1146
00:58:25,200 --> 00:58:29,340
Another task is to move forward.

1147
00:58:29,340 --> 00:58:32,390
If these are your different
tasks, you can get away with--

1148
00:58:32,390 --> 00:58:35,020
this is probably already a
very compact representation

1149
00:58:35,020 --> 00:58:37,400
because it's telling
you, are you turning?

1150
00:58:37,400 --> 00:58:38,940
Are you moving?

1151
00:58:38,940 --> 00:58:41,900
And so it's already telling
you about the relationship

1152
00:58:41,900 --> 00:58:43,320
between different tasks.

1153
00:58:43,320 --> 00:58:45,060
And so if your
task representation

1154
00:58:45,060 --> 00:58:48,020
is telling you about those
relationships, then--

1155
00:58:48,020 --> 00:58:50,740
or, for example, if you have
task representation that's,

1156
00:58:50,740 --> 00:58:54,380
like, the coordinates of
where you're trying to go,

1157
00:58:54,380 --> 00:58:56,140
then you're going to
have a task ID that's

1158
00:58:56,140 --> 00:58:58,420
very similar between
these two tasks.

1159
00:58:58,420 --> 00:59:00,700
And that will already
be quite compressed.

1160
00:59:00,700 --> 00:59:03,700
In those cases, you
can skip this step,

1161
00:59:03,700 --> 00:59:06,220
because those
representations will already

1162
00:59:06,220 --> 00:59:08,360
exclude irrelevant information.

1163
00:59:08,360 --> 00:59:10,760
But then if you instead have
a task ID that says one-hot

1164
00:59:10,760 --> 00:59:13,080
or that isn't very
informative, then you

1165
00:59:13,080 --> 00:59:16,240
need to compress that out.

1166
00:59:16,240 --> 00:59:20,473
And otherwise, there isn't--
this is one of the better

1167
00:59:20,473 --> 00:59:22,640
mathematical frameworks for
encouraging it to attend

1168
00:59:22,640 --> 00:59:24,120
to the relevant parts.

1169
00:59:24,120 --> 00:59:26,240
Yeah.

1170
00:59:26,240 --> 00:59:28,240
I guess in principle,
if you had a way

1171
00:59:28,240 --> 00:59:32,220
to change the way that this is
making decisions based on UI,

1172
00:59:32,220 --> 00:59:34,600
you might also be
able to do that.

1173
00:59:34,600 --> 00:59:37,340
Yeah, so what's the advantage
of outputting a distribution?

1174
00:59:37,340 --> 00:59:42,680
So the distribution here is
what allows us to compress.

1175
00:59:42,680 --> 00:59:46,600
And so by adding noise, or by
making this a stochastic neural

1176
00:59:46,600 --> 00:59:51,180
network, we're able to
remove information from it.

1177
00:59:51,180 --> 00:59:53,960
Whereas if you are
outputting-- if you're

1178
00:59:53,960 --> 00:59:59,128
passing a continuous
vector through,

1179
00:59:59,128 --> 01:00:01,680
there are heuristic
ways to try to compress,

1180
01:00:01,680 --> 01:00:04,970
for example, like by
compressing the dimensionality.

1181
01:00:04,970 --> 01:00:08,890
But you can still pass
infinite information

1182
01:00:08,890 --> 01:00:11,970
through even a one dimensional
continuous variable.

1183
01:00:11,970 --> 01:00:18,270
And so this is one way
that can, even in theory,

1184
01:00:18,270 --> 01:00:20,450
reduce information.

1185
01:00:20,450 --> 01:00:22,790
So now let's talk
about exploration.

1186
01:00:22,790 --> 01:00:27,250
So our goal in exploration is
to be able to try to predict z.

1187
01:00:27,250 --> 01:00:35,850
And I don't know
what notation I used.

1188
01:00:35,850 --> 01:00:38,890
So our goal is to try
to be able to collect

1189
01:00:38,890 --> 01:00:43,010
data that's predictive of z.

1190
01:00:43,010 --> 01:00:45,730
In practice, by the way, you
can train both of these policies

1191
01:00:45,730 --> 01:00:46,440
at the same time.

1192
01:00:46,440 --> 01:00:49,648

1193
01:00:49,648 --> 01:00:51,690
And we need to think about
how we would formulate

1194
01:00:51,690 --> 01:00:54,290
a reward function
that encourages

1195
01:00:54,290 --> 01:00:57,550
it to be predictive of z.

1196
01:00:57,550 --> 01:01:00,626

1197
01:01:00,626 --> 01:01:02,730
I don't know if--

1198
01:01:02,730 --> 01:01:04,000
I'll just pick some notation.

1199
01:01:04,000 --> 01:01:06,420
So say that we have--

1200
01:01:06,420 --> 01:01:09,820
we're going to be
learning some model that's

1201
01:01:09,820 --> 01:01:13,340
trying to be able to
predict z from the data

1202
01:01:13,340 --> 01:01:15,420
that we've collected.

1203
01:01:15,420 --> 01:01:19,820
And if we have some exploration
policy that's been exploring,

1204
01:01:19,820 --> 01:01:24,875
then we need to figure out
how to reward it accordingly.

1205
01:01:24,875 --> 01:01:27,500
And one thing that you could do
is you could collect some data,

1206
01:01:27,500 --> 01:01:29,680
and then at the end
of a trajectory,

1207
01:01:29,680 --> 01:01:33,020
for example, see how well
you can predict z and then

1208
01:01:33,020 --> 01:01:38,100
have the reward be the negative
prediction error for z.

1209
01:01:38,100 --> 01:01:40,780
and then zero everywhere else.

1210
01:01:40,780 --> 01:01:43,500
But if we did that, we'd
have a very sparse reward.

1211
01:01:43,500 --> 01:01:46,620
We'd only have it at the
end of the trajectory.

1212
01:01:46,620 --> 01:01:49,600
And what we can do is we
could actually incrementally,

1213
01:01:49,600 --> 01:01:51,820
at each time step, look
at how much information

1214
01:01:51,820 --> 01:01:54,300
we're gaining about z.

1215
01:01:54,300 --> 01:01:56,580
And that's going to give
us a more dense reward.

1216
01:01:56,580 --> 01:02:02,190
And so specifically, when we're
trying to train our exploration

1217
01:02:02,190 --> 01:02:11,240
policy, pi exploration, you can
think about the reward for St

1218
01:02:11,240 --> 01:02:13,830
At.

1219
01:02:13,830 --> 01:02:23,350
We could have it be the negative
prediction error times step t.

1220
01:02:23,350 --> 01:02:28,070
And that would be something
that's fairly reasonable.

1221
01:02:28,070 --> 01:02:34,270
But the action, like this
particular time step,

1222
01:02:34,270 --> 01:02:36,830
at this time step, we're
actually gaining just one time

1223
01:02:36,830 --> 01:02:38,510
step worth of information.

1224
01:02:38,510 --> 01:02:40,588
And if we wanted to
quantify that information,

1225
01:02:40,588 --> 01:02:42,630
we could actually look at
what was our prediction

1226
01:02:42,630 --> 01:02:45,470
error at the previous
time step, and look

1227
01:02:45,470 --> 01:02:48,910
at the relative difference in
the era of the previous time

1228
01:02:48,910 --> 01:02:51,050
step versus the error at
the current time step.

1229
01:02:51,050 --> 01:02:52,910
And that's how
much information we

1230
01:02:52,910 --> 01:02:56,870
gained from taking this action.

1231
01:02:56,870 --> 01:02:59,110
And so we could
define the reward

1232
01:02:59,110 --> 01:03:04,320
to be the prediction
error at t minus 1,

1233
01:03:04,320 --> 01:03:06,720
minus the prediction
error at time t.

1234
01:03:06,720 --> 01:03:09,560
And so how much?

1235
01:03:09,560 --> 01:03:13,200
This is something that should
be decreasing over time.

1236
01:03:13,200 --> 01:03:16,200
And this is basically
how better are we

1237
01:03:16,200 --> 01:03:18,920
at predicting zi
at the current time

1238
01:03:18,920 --> 01:03:22,280
step relative to the previous
time step, where prediction

1239
01:03:22,280 --> 01:03:23,920
error is something like--

1240
01:03:23,920 --> 01:03:28,530

1241
01:03:28,530 --> 01:03:35,480
is z hat minus the true zi.

1242
01:03:35,480 --> 01:03:38,720

1243
01:03:38,720 --> 01:03:41,960
And so we're
training this model.

1244
01:03:41,960 --> 01:03:44,460
We want this to be per
step information gain.

1245
01:03:44,460 --> 01:03:47,320
And so if we look
at all of the states

1246
01:03:47,320 --> 01:03:51,960
up until the current time
step versus all the time steps

1247
01:03:51,960 --> 01:03:55,600
up to the previous time step,
we can measure this notion

1248
01:03:55,600 --> 01:03:58,090
of information gain.

1249
01:03:58,090 --> 01:04:00,450
I guess maybe also to make
this a little bit more

1250
01:04:00,450 --> 01:04:03,930
clear, if this is
prediction error at time t,

1251
01:04:03,930 --> 01:04:15,290
then this means that z hat is
the estimate from Predicting z

1252
01:04:15,290 --> 01:04:21,290
from the data up until time t.

1253
01:04:21,290 --> 01:04:26,330
So everything in the
trajectory up until time t.

1254
01:04:26,330 --> 01:04:28,210
Cool.

1255
01:04:28,210 --> 01:04:30,870
So like many
research algorithms,

1256
01:04:30,870 --> 01:04:34,330
this has a somewhat
mangled, forced acronym.

1257
01:04:34,330 --> 01:04:36,610
So decoupled reward free
exploration and execution

1258
01:04:36,610 --> 01:04:38,290
in meta-RL, or DREAM.

1259
01:04:38,290 --> 01:04:44,010
And you'll be implementing this
algorithm in homework four.

1260
01:04:44,010 --> 01:04:50,730
So we talked before about
how if you train end-to-end,

1261
01:04:50,730 --> 01:04:52,847
if you train meta-RL
methods end-to-end,

1262
01:04:52,847 --> 01:04:54,930
and you're trying to
optimize for both exploration

1263
01:04:54,930 --> 01:04:57,340
and execution, you have
this coupling problem.

1264
01:04:57,340 --> 01:05:03,000
And the benefit is that you
can get optimal exploration.

1265
01:05:03,000 --> 01:05:07,220
And the downside is that
you're very, very inefficient.

1266
01:05:07,220 --> 01:05:09,380
And you can show
that this algorithm,

1267
01:05:09,380 --> 01:05:12,260
under fairly mild
assumptions, will actually

1268
01:05:12,260 --> 01:05:15,000
recover optimal exploration
and exploitation.

1269
01:05:15,000 --> 01:05:17,505

1270
01:05:17,505 --> 01:05:19,380
And then if you look at
the efficiency, which

1271
01:05:19,380 --> 01:05:21,620
is where end-to-end
optimization struggled,

1272
01:05:21,620 --> 01:05:24,040
you can think about it.

1273
01:05:24,040 --> 01:05:27,312
You can construct a simple
bandit problem, where--

1274
01:05:27,312 --> 01:05:31,860
a bandit-like problem, where
you have these different arms.

1275
01:05:31,860 --> 01:05:37,080
And arm zero, instead of
being a typical action,

1276
01:05:37,080 --> 01:05:41,220
is going to tell you what
is the optimal action.

1277
01:05:41,220 --> 01:05:43,780
And so if you, for
example, learn the strategy

1278
01:05:43,780 --> 01:05:48,020
of taking action zero, and
then taking the optimal action,

1279
01:05:48,020 --> 01:05:52,080
then that's like optimally
exploring and then exploiting.

1280
01:05:52,080 --> 01:05:56,310

1281
01:05:56,310 --> 01:05:58,490
And in an end-to-end
optimization,

1282
01:05:58,490 --> 01:06:01,670
you can actually quantify
how many samples it needs

1283
01:06:01,670 --> 01:06:03,350
for the meta learning process.

1284
01:06:03,350 --> 01:06:04,850
You get something
like A squared log

1285
01:06:04,850 --> 01:06:08,590
A, where A is the number of
arms or the number of actions,

1286
01:06:08,590 --> 01:06:12,110
whereas the DREAM algorithm
requires only A log A. There's

1287
01:06:12,110 --> 01:06:14,670
some assumptions here in
terms of using Q-learning,

1288
01:06:14,670 --> 01:06:20,870
using uniform exploration in
the outer loop of the algorithm.

1289
01:06:20,870 --> 01:06:24,350
And you can look at,
basically, the efficiency,

1290
01:06:24,350 --> 01:06:28,210
like how efficient
is the algorithm.

1291
01:06:28,210 --> 01:06:32,190
You can see that
DREAM is able to get--

1292
01:06:32,190 --> 01:06:34,790
is able to reach the optimal
behavior in many, many fewer

1293
01:06:34,790 --> 01:06:38,390
samples than if you were to use
end-to-end optimization, which

1294
01:06:38,390 --> 01:06:42,750
is shown in orange as a
function of the number of arms.

1295
01:06:42,750 --> 01:06:48,830
So as the problem gets
more and more complicated,

1296
01:06:48,830 --> 01:06:50,830
DREAM is able to
do far, far better

1297
01:06:50,830 --> 01:06:54,120
than end-to-end optimization.

1298
01:06:54,120 --> 01:06:55,900
We can also empirically
look at things.

1299
01:06:55,900 --> 01:06:58,520
So here's an example of
a meta-RL problem where

1300
01:06:58,520 --> 01:07:03,560
different tasks correspond
to going to different objects

1301
01:07:03,560 --> 01:07:04,960
with different colors.

1302
01:07:04,960 --> 01:07:08,778
And the color of the object
is written on the wall.

1303
01:07:08,778 --> 01:07:10,320
And so and the agent
starts over here

1304
01:07:10,320 --> 01:07:13,280
and it needs to walk around
the wall, walk around the wall,

1305
01:07:13,280 --> 01:07:17,140
read the color, and then
go to the correct object.

1306
01:07:17,140 --> 01:07:19,560
So it's exploring and then--
or seeking information,

1307
01:07:19,560 --> 01:07:21,800
and then exploiting.

1308
01:07:21,800 --> 01:07:23,660
It gets pixel rewards.

1309
01:07:23,660 --> 01:07:28,960
And if you compare end-to-end
algorithms that are shown here,

1310
01:07:28,960 --> 01:07:32,560
they really, really struggle
to do this end-to-end learning

1311
01:07:32,560 --> 01:07:34,960
to explore problem.

1312
01:07:34,960 --> 01:07:37,280
If you use one of the
alternative strategies

1313
01:07:37,280 --> 01:07:39,880
that we talked
about on Wednesday,

1314
01:07:39,880 --> 01:07:41,820
you can do better, in theory.

1315
01:07:41,820 --> 01:07:45,640
But you still have a suboptimal
exploration strategy.

1316
01:07:45,640 --> 01:07:47,280
Whereas with this
algorithm, you're

1317
01:07:47,280 --> 01:07:51,450
able to actually learn how
to explore, go find the sign,

1318
01:07:51,450 --> 01:07:53,730
and then go to
the correct object

1319
01:07:53,730 --> 01:07:58,610
by actually trying to
decouple exploration

1320
01:07:58,610 --> 01:08:02,330
for identifying the task,
and exploitation of solving

1321
01:08:02,330 --> 01:08:04,050
the task.

1322
01:08:04,050 --> 01:08:04,690
Cool.

1323
01:08:04,690 --> 01:08:06,148
And then you can
actually see this.

1324
01:08:06,148 --> 01:08:08,850
So here's a video of
the exploration episode

1325
01:08:08,850 --> 01:08:12,130
where it's walking
around and going

1326
01:08:12,130 --> 01:08:16,109
to read the sign on the wall
in this simple environment.

1327
01:08:16,109 --> 01:08:18,330
And then after it
reads the sign,

1328
01:08:18,330 --> 01:08:21,890
it's able to go to the blue key.

1329
01:08:21,890 --> 01:08:23,310
Yeah, that's a great question.

1330
01:08:23,310 --> 01:08:26,750
So the question is,
this is a setting where

1331
01:08:26,750 --> 01:08:29,250
exploration and exploitation,
you can first explore and then

1332
01:08:29,250 --> 01:08:29,950
exploit.

1333
01:08:29,950 --> 01:08:31,689
You might also have
tasks where you

1334
01:08:31,689 --> 01:08:33,870
need to interleave
exploration and exploitation,

1335
01:08:33,870 --> 01:08:36,270
where you first need to
find some information,

1336
01:08:36,270 --> 01:08:38,490
then use that to
accomplish a subtask

1337
01:08:38,490 --> 01:08:41,689
and then find an
exploration again.

1338
01:08:41,689 --> 01:08:43,910
We have done some work
that tries to combine them.

1339
01:08:43,910 --> 01:08:46,090
And you can, for example,
train a single policy

1340
01:08:46,090 --> 01:08:49,710
that uses the reward to identify
the task combined with the task

1341
01:08:49,710 --> 01:08:51,590
reward.

1342
01:08:51,590 --> 01:08:54,270
And if you're interested, I can
point you to a relevant work

1343
01:08:54,270 --> 01:08:56,402
there.

1344
01:08:56,402 --> 01:09:00,390
So to summarize
learning to explore

1345
01:09:00,390 --> 01:09:02,630
and learning policies
that know how to explore,

1346
01:09:02,630 --> 01:09:07,470
end-to-end can learn optimal
exploration and exploitation.

1347
01:09:07,470 --> 01:09:11,090
But if exploration is hard,
it's pretty hard to optimize.

1348
01:09:11,090 --> 01:09:14,430
And we saw an example of
that, where all of the methods

1349
01:09:14,430 --> 01:09:18,550
were kind of flatlining and
not learning good exploration.

1350
01:09:18,550 --> 01:09:20,670
Alternative strategies
are easier to optimize

1351
01:09:20,670 --> 01:09:23,029
and can be principled,
but can also

1352
01:09:23,029 --> 01:09:25,790
be suboptimal by large
amounts, and be very

1353
01:09:25,790 --> 01:09:28,710
inefficient about exploration.

1354
01:09:28,710 --> 01:09:32,109
And this decoupled approach
leads to the optimal strategy,

1355
01:09:32,109 --> 01:09:33,330
is also easy to optimize.

1356
01:09:33,330 --> 01:09:35,830
So it sort of gets the
best of both worlds.

1357
01:09:35,830 --> 01:09:38,609
It does require this
task identifier.

1358
01:09:38,609 --> 01:09:40,830
You need to at least be
able to enumerate your tasks

1359
01:09:40,830 --> 01:09:43,229
and know which of
those tasks you're in.

1360
01:09:43,229 --> 01:09:46,439
And that's the key thing
that it's using in order

1361
01:09:46,439 --> 01:09:49,160
to get the benefits.

1362
01:09:49,160 --> 01:09:50,265
Cool.

1363
01:09:50,265 --> 01:09:52,640
We have a little bit of time,
and so you could actually--

1364
01:09:52,640 --> 01:09:54,660
I wanted to briefly talk
about one application.

1365
01:09:54,660 --> 01:09:56,795
So you can take this
algorithm and use it

1366
01:09:56,795 --> 01:09:58,920
in a domain that's a little
bit different from what

1367
01:09:58,920 --> 01:10:00,640
we've seen before.

1368
01:10:00,640 --> 01:10:04,960
And this domain is trying to
find bugs in different computer

1369
01:10:04,960 --> 01:10:10,112
programs, and in particular, in
intro computer science courses,

1370
01:10:10,112 --> 01:10:12,320
although this would probably
apply to other programs,

1371
01:10:12,320 --> 01:10:14,200
as well.

1372
01:10:14,200 --> 01:10:15,880
It's really cool
for students to be

1373
01:10:15,880 --> 01:10:19,760
able to make computer programs.

1374
01:10:19,760 --> 01:10:22,000
It's very rewarding for
them to write a program

1375
01:10:22,000 --> 01:10:23,640
as they're learning how to code.

1376
01:10:23,640 --> 01:10:26,460
Then that creates a
program like this.

1377
01:10:26,460 --> 01:10:30,120
So on code.org, students
code up this game

1378
01:10:30,120 --> 01:10:34,320
called Bounce that looks a bit
like Pong, or one side of Pong,

1379
01:10:34,320 --> 01:10:37,880
where the goal is to hit
a ball into the goal.

1380
01:10:37,880 --> 01:10:41,720
In CS106A at Stanford,
students code up Breakout,

1381
01:10:41,720 --> 01:10:44,420
and so they might code
up a program like this.

1382
01:10:44,420 --> 01:10:49,410
This is an example of a buggy
game, where the ball actually

1383
01:10:49,410 --> 01:10:51,553
kind of gets skewered
by the paddle.

1384
01:10:51,553 --> 01:10:52,970
It kind of bounces
off the paddle,

1385
01:10:52,970 --> 01:10:57,090
rather than going and losing
a point or losing a life.

1386
01:10:57,090 --> 01:11:00,290
And we can basically treat
different student programs

1387
01:11:00,290 --> 01:11:04,170
as different tasks, where our
goal is to explore and find

1388
01:11:04,170 --> 01:11:06,170
the bugs that might exist.

1389
01:11:06,170 --> 01:11:08,090
And our ultimate
goal is to then be

1390
01:11:08,090 --> 01:11:10,770
able to provide feedback to the
student on the different bugs

1391
01:11:10,770 --> 01:11:12,673
in their program.

1392
01:11:12,673 --> 01:11:14,090
One of the other
things about this

1393
01:11:14,090 --> 01:11:15,727
is it's really time
consuming for TAs

1394
01:11:15,727 --> 01:11:17,810
or instructors to actually
grade and give feedback

1395
01:11:17,810 --> 01:11:19,435
on these assignments,
because you often

1396
01:11:19,435 --> 01:11:23,110
end up playing the game in order
to figure out and find the bugs.

1397
01:11:23,110 --> 01:11:23,610
Cool.

1398
01:11:23,610 --> 01:11:24,850
So we can use meta-RL.

1399
01:11:24,850 --> 01:11:27,250
We can actually use
exactly the DREAM algorithm

1400
01:11:27,250 --> 01:11:30,450
and train it to be
able to identify

1401
01:11:30,450 --> 01:11:35,450
different distinguishing
factors from different students.

1402
01:11:35,450 --> 01:11:37,130
And it can learn
exploration strategies,

1403
01:11:37,130 --> 01:11:39,470
like what happens if
the ball hits the goal.

1404
01:11:39,470 --> 01:11:41,970
So here, it's trying to actually
play the game successfully.

1405
01:11:41,970 --> 01:11:44,875
It can also check and
act, so what happens

1406
01:11:44,875 --> 01:11:46,000
if the ball hits the floor.

1407
01:11:46,000 --> 01:11:49,100
So it's actually purposely
trying to not hit the ball,

1408
01:11:49,100 --> 01:11:51,780
or also try to hit the
wall instead of the goal.

1409
01:11:51,780 --> 01:11:53,740
And this is an example
of a buggy program that

1410
01:11:53,740 --> 01:11:57,060
creates lots of different
balls as soon as the ball hits

1411
01:11:57,060 --> 01:12:00,140
the wall.

1412
01:12:00,140 --> 01:12:04,020
And then there was an
experiment in 106A two years ago

1413
01:12:04,020 --> 01:12:08,060
in the spring 2020 quarter,
where TAs actually used

1414
01:12:08,060 --> 01:12:11,820
the meta-RL algorithm in the
policy to basically grade

1415
01:12:11,820 --> 01:12:14,780
and pre-populate the
rubric for grading.

1416
01:12:14,780 --> 01:12:17,540
And then they just verified
that the rubric answers

1417
01:12:17,540 --> 01:12:20,820
were correct from watching the
videos of the meta-RL agent

1418
01:12:20,820 --> 01:12:22,580
playing the game.

1419
01:12:22,580 --> 01:12:26,660
And it led to 44% faster
grading, and also 6% more

1420
01:12:26,660 --> 01:12:30,460
accurate grading, and the
TAs generally liked using it.

1421
01:12:30,460 --> 01:12:32,300
They also used it
in at least one

1422
01:12:32,300 --> 01:12:37,530
subsequent quarter because
they liked using it so much.

1423
01:12:37,530 --> 01:12:42,000

